shared date-keyed filename; fixed by giving each mode its own output path | 2026-09-02.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/handoff-doc-uncommitted-tooling-changes]] | Handoff with `memlint.py` and four supporting files left uncommitted by choice, a temp-path placement recurrence, and a cross-session \"another session may have fixed it\" check | 2026-09-02.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[connections/always-loaded-context-is-the-expensive-place-to-be-wrong]] | Wrongness, staleness, and volume are three costs on the same session-resident surface; consolidation concentrates the first unless the pointer's target is re-verified | 2026-09-02.md, 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/microsoft-forms-vs-google-forms-inventory-mismatch]] | Live inventory survey runs on Microsoft Forms while project docs point at 08-13-era Google Forms; a `gws` query there returns a truthful-but-false \"no new responses\", exposed only by the raw `.xlsx` header row | 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/tier-a-memory-as-the-durable-payload-of-a-verification-session]] | A verification check's payload is a trap plus a command, so it routes to Tier A memory rather than a handoff doc; reinforced by the project not being a git repo | 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[connections/a-stale-pointer-resolves-cleanly-to-the-wrong-answer]] | Stale Google Form IDs, a stale DEV memory router, and a nearly-dangling AGENTS.md pointer all succeed at resolution while returning the wrong answer; check from the target's side and stamp a verification date | 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] | Adds the unpushed `dc54c97`/`38e462b` commits and the cross-link to `DexaProject` still living as a public GitHub repo with a patient CSV | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] | Adds the 78→86 job-count drift from 2026-09-15, restating the tell in its threshold-free form (single-digit okCount = SSD absent) | 2026-09-09.md, 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/error-log-archive-collision-overwrite]] | A `cp` to a date-keyed archive path destroyed three prior `.errors.log` lines (recovered from git); archive names must not collide or must append; `cp /dev/null` is the only working truncation route | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/session-transcript-tracking-repo-growth]] | Tracking session transcripts added ~368 MB to `~/.claude` and made repo size grow daily; left as an open decision because Tier B capture is a proven reconstruction fallback | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[connections/the-preservation-step-must-itself-be-verified]] | Archive-before-clear and free-run-overwrites-paid both failed by encoding a once-daily assumption into a filename; the verified tarball discipline shows why an unchecked safety net is worse than none | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/dev-thin-repo-github-consolidation]] | `~/DEV` converted to thin repo `erash11/DEV` tracking 12 router files; `/*` block-all `.gitignore` as the load-bearing line, `CLAUDE.md` deliberately kept as a workspace map, Windows box confirmed active | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/public-repo-privacy-audit-read-inside]] | 21 public repos read inside: only `B_Ellis` and `cmj-norms-app` held real athlete data; 404-as-live bug and a Vercel-not-Pages miss in the liveness check; 18 flipped private | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] | 36 Windows/Mac conflicts collapse to 4 (26 CRLF-only, 6 Mac-newer generated); \"Mac wins\" would have discarded 324 unique forecast-log rows | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[connections/name-and-flag-are-not-the-artifact]] | Repo names, conflict flags, and cleaned exports all over- and under-include at once; treat the cheap list as a work queue, classify by kind before resolving | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]] | Revisions supersede, accumulating records diverge; the correct verb is merge or append, and a smaller newer copy is the tell | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts]] | ~200 `.from-*` conflicts traced to a Windows save command mangling encoding and prepending BOMs; proved on `portfolio.json` (9 updates matched, nothing lost), root cause fixed, 95 BOM files left alone | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/backup-oom-kill-during-first-time-index]] | Backup OOM-killed during a 20-min first-time index of `dev-docs` (547 files); index stale not corrupt, two dead-PID locks cleared, CPU% rejected as a health signal | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/backup-manifest-symlink-false-alarm]] | `manifest-unmanaged:_DRIVE` was a symlink, not a missing repo; backup taught to skip symlinks, two real gaps added with a 0 lost / 0 changed / 2 added proof | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]] | Four \"not a repo\" folders were parent-tracked, five \"missing\" repos were name mismatches, two `repo-diverged` errors had self-cleared; verify before `gh repo create` | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/git-submodule-branch-pinning-decision]] | `ZoteroInjestion` made a real submodule but pinned to `mac-snapshot-2026-09-09` rather than `main`; a snapshot pin decays silently, decision deferred | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] | Mangled sync saves, a symlink-blind manifest check, and a backup committing its own output: recurrence is the tell that the fix belongs in the generator | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[connections/a-killed-process-leaves-debris-that-blocks-the-retry]] | Dead-PID locks and orphaned indexers are not self-healing; a loud crash becomes an indefinitely quiet no-op unless the kill's leavings are enumerated | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/harness-agnostic-aios-plan]] | Multi-task Mac+Windows skills/backup plan: 42 own vs 19 third-party skills reframing, Task 4 inversion script deleted, Task 2 prune 306→208 as a reversible move, three open round-10 findings | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/gitignore-trailing-slash-misses-symlinks]] | A `.gitignore` pattern ending in `/` matches directories but not symlinks, so the rule silently no-ops and an automated backup re-adds the entries; plus `.gitignore` vs `.git/info/exclude` scope | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/backup-verification-by-restore]] | Restore test returned 57 of 61 skills, exposing a two-hop symlink chain into an unbacked-up `~/.agents/vendor-review/`; only an actual restore can measure coverage the backup's own records cannot | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/cross-provider-review-gate]] | A rate-limit-forced switch from Codex to Claude found the plan's biggest error; resume cannot cross providers, and a prior approval is stale after an amendment (5 new bugs in the amendment itself) | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/submodule-conversion-breaks-rsync-backup]] | Submodule conversion turned `.git` from directory to file; rsync cannot overwrite a destination directory with a file, failing only while the SSD is mounted; fixed by rename, `okCount: 80` | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[connections/a-type-change-at-the-source-breaks-the-mirror]] | Adds the Codex-skills symlink refusal as a second rsync type-change instance, making recurrence the argument for fixing the tooling | 2026-09-12.md, 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[connections/change-the-checker-down-or-sideways]] | Replacing a failed check with a primitive substrate works only where a ground truth exists; otherwise the only lever is a peer with uncorrelated blind spots. | adjudicated | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[connections/the-execution-context-silently-edits-the-work-set]] | Session lifetime and mount state both silently shrink what a run attempts without producing an error, and both are fixed by moving the verdict onto a durable artifact that publishes cardinality. | adjudicated | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[connections/test-the-layer-before-blaming-the-artifact]] | A failed upload or render usually indicts the transport layer, not the file — hold the artifact fixed and remove a stage before rebuilding anything | adjudicated | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/windows-backup-recovery-inventory]] | Windows laptop had no working backup: ~15 GB single-copy, nine Disabled tasks, two triggerless ones, and the Mac's manifest installed as Windows'; `/MIR`→`/E` fix | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/windows-backup-task-trigger-gotchas]] | Four Task Scheduler gotchas: `schtasks /Change` can't add a schedule, triggerless tasks show `Ready` forever, `G:` is per-user so SYSTEM writes nothing, sleeping laptops miss fixed-time triggers | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/fix-from-the-working-machine-handoff]] | Partition cross-machine repair by capability: author all file edits from the working machine, hand the broken one a paste-ready instruction page and in-file \"nothing reads this yet\" notes | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/verify-the-diagnosis-in-a-received-report]] | A received recovery report's findings held but one explanation didn't: `dev-sync` skips an enumerated list, not underscore-prefixed folders, so `_INBOX` was already a clean pushed repo | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/unattended-cross-machine-push-collisions]] | Three Windows pushes mid-session each needing a merge, including a deliberate-looking 913-file skill audit cutting ~160→63; accepted because git retains deletions, intent still to be confirmed | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[connections/every-layer-reported-healthy-except-the-destination]] | `Ready`, `status: OK`, and a backup's own records were each locally truthful while coverage failed; narrow stage predicates don't compose into end-to-end assurance | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/rsync-symlink-conversion-stale-directory]] | Codex skills converted to symlinks while the SSD held real directories; rsync cannot replace a directory with a symlink, fixed by moving four folders to `_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-2026-09-15/` | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/manifest-classification-not-inferable-from-names]] | Six Baylor-sounding repos (`VALD_Performance`, `RTP_ProgressReport`, …) were personal, not work; manifest classification must be asked, not inferred | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/clear-the-error-log-only-after-a-clean-run]] | `.errors.log` archived and cleared only after a verified 0-error/86-job re-run — fixes are not their own confirmation | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/vendored-copy-drift-within-a-repo]] | `LibraryProject`'s embedded `ZoteroInjestion` one commit behind the standalone repo; `STATUS.md` missing its Next section with an unmerged Windows copy pasted at the bottom | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n\n\n## Related Wiki Articles\n\n### concepts/windows-backup-recovery-inventory.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Windows Laptop Backup Failure and RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md\"\naliases: [RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md, Windows no working backup, ~15 GB single copy, nine disabled KeystoneOS tasks, Mac manifest on Windows, FIX-WINDOWS-BACKUP.md, backup_dev.ps1 MIR to E]\ntags: [backup, windows, macos, automation, keystoneos, scheduling, data-integrity, migration]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-14.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-14\nupdated: 2026-09-14\n---\n\n# Windows Laptop Backup Failure and RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md\n\nOn 2026-09-14 a `RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md` commit arriving from the Windows laptop revealed that the machine had **no working backup at all**. Roughly 15 GB exists in a single copy only — `SportScienceResearchRepo` (4.37 GB), `Football` (2.94 GB), `Risk_Stratification_Engine` (1.80 GB), `ForcePlate_DecisionSystem` (1.69 GB), and the `~/.codex` history. Everything else — all 131 repos, `~/.claude`, skills, global memory, and Google Drive documents — is safe. The failure had three independent root causes, all of them artifacts of the Mac migration.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Nine KeystoneOS Windows scheduled tasks are Disabled** — switched off during the move to the Mac and never restored. They report their disabled state accurately; nothing ever escalated it.\n- **Two replacement tasks were registered with no trigger.** A triggerless task shows `Ready` indefinitely and will never fire — the healthiest-looking possible state for a job that does nothing.\n- **The Windows backup manifest is the Mac's manifest.** It carries `/Volumes/CRASHBOX` and `/Users/kristinarash/...` paths, with its last state written 2026-08-14 at status ERROR. `~/.claude/scripts/daily-backup.ps1` had likewise been overwritten by the Mac version (rsync + Mac paths) on 2026-08-05; the original is frozen at `daily-backup.ps1.win-orig`.\n- **`backup_dev.ps1` was changed from `/MIR` to `/E`.** Mirroring propagates accidental deletions into the backup, which converts the backup into a second copy of a mistake rather than a recovery path. (`backup_ai_sessions.ps1` was already `/E`.)\n- **Two deliberate non-actions:** the backup scripts stay in `DEV\\_INBOX\\` because the registered scheduled task points there, and the nine disabled tasks are *not* re-enabled because the script they invoke is now the Mac's.\n\n## Details\n\nWhat makes this a distinct failure rather than another manifest-drift instance is that every layer reported truthfully and none of them reported the operational fact. A disabled task is disabled; a triggerless task is genuinely ready; a manifest containing Mac paths is a syntactically valid manifest. The only signal that would have surfaced the problem is the one nobody was checking: whether bytes actually landed in Google Drive. This is the same scope mismatch catalogued in [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]], where `launchctl list` reports only the last *scheduled* run — the field answers a narrower predicate than the operator's question and is therefore silent about the gap. The recovery report's own conclusion states the rule directly: verification must confirm bytes at the destination, not that the task looks healthy.\n\nThe three causes also share a single origin. Tasks disabled \"during the Mac move,\" a manifest overwritten with Mac paths on 08-14, a `daily-backup.ps1` overwritten on 08-05 — the migration edited the Windows machine's automation surface as a side effect of syncing shared files, and nothing on the Windows side was left running to notice. This extends the pattern in [[connections/windows-defaults-outlive-the-windows-machine]] in the opposite direction: that article covers Windows assumptions surviving onto the Mac, whereas here Mac-shaped configuration flowed back onto Windows through the same shared repositories. Bidirectional sync between two machines with platform-specific automation means any file that encodes host facts — paths, mount points, shell dialects — is a collision waiting to happen, which is why the new `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.windows.json` was created as a *separate* file rather than a re-edit of the shared one.\n\nThe `/MIR` → `/E` change deserves isolation as policy. Robocopy's `/MIR` makes the destination an exact mirror of the source, which means a local deletion is faithfully reproduced in the backup at the next run — the backup is then a copy of the current state rather than a record of history. `/E` copies without deleting, accepting stale files in exchange for never propagating a loss. The trade-off resolves the same way as the append-only forecast log in [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]]: when the artifact's value is that it holds something the source might no longer have, the correct verb is never \"make identical.\"\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/windows-backup-task-trigger-gotchas]] — The four scheduling mechanics that make a Windows backup task silently ineffective, and the paste-ready fix page.\n- [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]] — `Ready`, `Disabled`, and a valid-but-wrong manifest all report accurately on a narrower predicate than \"is the data backed up?\"\n- [[connections/windows-defaults-outlive-the-windows-machine]] — The mirror-image case: platform-specific configuration surviving a migration, here flowing Mac → Windows.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] — The Mac-side manifest whose paths ended up on the Windows machine.\n- [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] — The companion lesson that a backup's own status field cannot establish coverage.\n- [[connections/the-execution-context-silently-edits-the-work-set]] — A triggerless task and a SYSTEM-context missing `G:` both shrink the work set without producing an error.\n- [[concepts/crashbox-ssd-backup-baseline]] — The Mac-side SSD leg; its `/backup` run was still outstanding at session close.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-14.md]] — Memory maintenance session (22:05): `RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md` arriving from the Windows laptop; ~15 GB in single copy only (SportScienceResearchRepo 4.37 GB, Football 2.94 GB, Risk_Stratification_Engine 1.80 GB, ForcePlate_DecisionSystem 1.69 GB, `~/.codex` history) with 131 repos, `~/.claude`, skills, global memory, and Drive docs safe; three root causes (nine Disabled KeystoneOS tasks, two triggerless replacement tasks showing `Ready`, Windows manifest actually the Mac's with `/Volumes/CRASHBOX` and `/Users/kristinarash/...` paths at status ERROR 2026-08-14); `backup_dev.ps1` changed `/MIR` → `/E`; `backup-manifest.windows.json` created as documentation only; scripts kept in `DEV\\_INBOX\\` and the nine tasks left disabled because `daily-backup.ps1` was overwritten by the Mac version on 2026-08-05 (original at `daily-backup.ps1.win-orig`).\n\n```\n\n### concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Claude Memory Compiler Setup\"\naliases: [claude-memory-compiler, coleam00/claude-memory-compiler, persistent memory hooks, session-start session-end pre-compact, memory compiler version skew, claude-agent-sdk version skew, FLUSH_ERROR version skew, uv lock upgrade]\ntags: [claude-code, memory, automation, infrastructure, hooks, maintenance]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-14.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-05-19.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-13.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-14\nupdated: 2026-06-16\n---\n\n# Claude Memory Compiler Setup\n\nOn 2026-04-14, the `coleam00/claude-memory-compiler` system — the same daily-log-to-knowledge-base architecture this very knowledge base runs on — was installed to give Claude Code persistent memory across sessions. The repo was cloned to `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/`, Python dependencies installed via `uv sync`, and the knowledge base directory structure (`daily/`, `knowledge/concepts/`, etc.) initialized. This system is Tier C of a three-tier Claude Code memory architecture; see [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] for the full picture including Tier A (native auto-memory) and Tier B (custom Stop-hook scratchpad). On 2026-06-13, a day-long failure caused by `claude-agent-sdk` version skew revealed an important operational maintenance pattern.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Three global hooks were wired into `~/.claude/settings.json`: `session-start.py` (SessionStart), `session-end.py` (SessionEnd), and `pre-compact.py` (PreCompact).\n- Hook commands invoke `uv` via its absolute path (`~/.local/bin/uv`) rather than the bare `uv` command, because Claude Code's hook execution environment may not include `~/.local/bin` on `PATH`.\n- All script invocations follow the `uv run --directory ~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler <script>` pattern, guaranteeing the correct virtualenv context regardless of the current working directory when a hook fires.\n- Hook configuration changes are not picked up live — they require a full Claude Code restart (or a `/hooks` reload) before they take effect.\n- After 6 PM local time, the `flush.py` background process automatically triggers `compile.py` to turn that day's daily log into knowledge articles, without manual intervention. Manual queries are also available via `uv run --directory ~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler python scripts/query.py \"<question>\"`.\n- **The agent itself never receives a \"session ending\" notification** — SessionEnd and PreCompact are harness-level events; the harness runs the hook shell commands, not the agent. Teardown logic must live in the hook, not in agent-side instructions.\n- **Version skew failure mode:** the memory compiler's `uv` virtualenv pins `claude-agent-sdk` to a specific version, which bundles a specific Claude Code CLI binary. When the system Claude Code auto-updates (e.g., from 2.1.92 to 2.1.177), the bundled CLI in the venv falls behind and eventually cannot parse the current `~/.claude/settings.json`, causing every `flush.py` invocation to exit immediately with a non-zero code. Symptom: 100% FLUSH_ERROR rate after a Claude Code update, despite no changes to the memory compiler itself.\n- **Fix for version skew:** `cd ~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler && uv lock --upgrade-package claude-agent-sdk && uv sync`. This upgrades only the `claude-agent-sdk` dependency and its bundled CLI to match the current system version without touching other pinned dependencies. Run this whenever FLUSH_ERRORs appear after a Claude Code auto-update.\n- **Diagnostic caution:** do NOT invoke the bundled `claude.exe` CLI directly to diagnose version skew — it spawns a real nested agent session. Instead, inspect venv package metadata (e.g., `uv pip show claude-agent-sdk`) to check the installed version against the system CLI version.\n\n## Details\n\nThe setup session focused on making the memory pipeline reliable rather than merely functional. The PATH issue with `uv` is a generalizable lesson for any Claude Code hook that shells out to a tool installed via `uv`, `pipx`, or similar user-local installers: hook processes may run with a minimal environment, so absolute paths (or explicit `PATH` exports) are safer than relying on the interactive shell's configuration. Similarly, the `uv run --directory` pattern avoids a whole class of \"wrong virtualenv\" bugs that would otherwise occur if a hook fires while Claude Code's working directory is some unrelated project.\n\nOnce restarted, the three hooks form a closed loop: `session-start.py` injects the current knowledge index and the most recent daily log into every new session's context (capped at 20,000 characters), `session-end.py` and `pre-compact.py` capture the raw conversation transcript and spawn a detached `flush.py` process, and `flush.py` decides what's worth appending to the day's daily log and, after 6 PM, kicks off `compile.py` to synthesize that log into structured `knowledge/` articles. PreCompact serves as a safety net for long sessions: in a session with multiple auto-compactions, PreCompact fires before each one, ensuring at least one compiled log exists even if the session later crashes before SessionEnd fires.\n\nThe 2026-06-13 version skew failure is operationally important. On that day, Claude Code auto-updated from approximately version 2.1.92 to 2.1.177 — 85 releases ahead — while the memory compiler's venv still bundled the older CLI via `claude-agent-sdk==0.1.56`. The newer `~/.claude/settings.json` included hooks, settings, or schemas added after version 2.1.92 that the stale bundled CLI could not parse; every `flush.py` call exited on startup without processing the session. The failure was isolated entirely to the Tier C compile layer: Tier B (the `transcript-capture.js` Stop-hook) continued capturing sessions uninterrupted because it has no dependency on `claude-agent-sdk`. Twenty-four days of continuous Tier B logs and four sessions captured on the failure day itself confirmed the architectural independence. This means that during a version-skew failure, raw session context is preserved in Tier B transcripts and can be backfilled after the fix is applied. The `uv lock --upgrade-package claude-agent-sdk && uv sync` fix is surgical — it upgrades only the affected package without touching other pinned dependencies — and is safe to apply at any time. A post-fix test flush verifies the fix before trusting that normal operation has resumed. See [[connections/memory-compiler-tier-independence-under-failure]] for the architectural insight this failure demonstrated empirically.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — The three-tier framing that situates this compiler as Tier C, alongside Tier A (native auto-memory) and Tier B (custom Stop-hook per-turn scratchpad).\n- [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] — A later (2026-06-10) maintenance pass on the same Claude Code environment, addressing plugin/MCP configuration drift in the broader tooling this memory system runs inside.\n- [[concepts/daily-onramp-automation]] — Another automation pipeline (built 2026-06-10) sharing the same token-efficiency motivation: pre-process inputs so the LLM only has to read a compact summary.\n- [[connections/memory-compiler-tier-independence-under-failure]] — Documents the 2026-06-13 empirical demonstration that Tier B (transcript capture) remained fully operational through a day-long Tier C (compiler) failure, validating the architecture's independence claim under real failure conditions.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-04-14.md]] — Initial install, hook wiring, and PATH/uv lessons from the setup session.\n- [[daily/2026-05-19.md]] — Clarification of hook firing mechanics and the \"agent never sees session ending\" architectural detail (16:53).\n- [[daily/2026-06-13.md]] — Version skew failure and fix session (17:26 Memory Flush): root cause (claude-agent-sdk==0.1.56 bundling CLI 2.1.92 vs system 2.1.177), Tier B independence confirmed (24 days continuous logs), fix via `uv lock --upgrade-package claude-agent-sdk && uv sync`, diagnostic caution about invoking bundled claude.exe.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"DEV Sync False Alarms: Parent-Tracked Folders and Name-Mismatched Repos\"\naliases: [not a repo false alarm, verify before gh repo create, parent repo tracks subfolder, five missing repos name mismatch, Linda Projects path with spaces, parallel fetch loop breaks on spaces, self-cleared repo-diverged]\ntags: [git, sync, workflow, verification, macos, gotcha, github]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-11.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-11\nupdated: 2026-09-12\n---\n\n# DEV Sync False Alarms: Parent-Tracked Folders and Name-Mismatched Repos\n\nA 2026-09-11 `/dev-sync` run reconciling `~/DEV` against the `erash11` GitHub account produced three categories of finding that all evaporated on inspection: four folders flagged as \"not a repo\" were tracked by a parent repository, five repos that appeared missing locally were name mismatches or config repos, and two `repo-diverged` session-start errors had already self-cleared. Nothing was pulled, and the correct conclusion was that nothing was behind.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **\"Not a repo\" from a directory walk usually means \"tracked by a parent repo.\"** A subdirectory of a git repository has no `.git` of its own and will fail a per-folder repo test while being fully version-controlled.\n- **Verify before running `gh repo create`.** Acting on the flag would have created four redundant GitHub repositories and introduced nested-repo hazards where none existed.\n- **Apparently-missing remote repos were name mismatches or config repos**, not absent work — five in total, none requiring action.\n- **The `repo-diverged:global-memory` / `repo-diverged:dotclaude` errors had self-cleared** by the time they were investigated: both repos showed 0 ahead / 0 behind. Session-start errors are a snapshot of a moment that may already have passed.\n- **\"Nothing was behind\" was confirmed by reasoning, not assumed:** Windows last synced 9/9 and all work since has been on the Mac, so an empty pull is the consistent outcome rather than a suspicious one.\n\n## Details\n\nThe unifying property across all three false alarms is that a cheap automated check produced an accurate answer to a question slightly narrower than the one the operator was actually asking. \"Does this directory contain a `.git`?\" is not \"is this directory's content under version control,\" and the gap between them is exactly a subdirectory of a tracked parent. \"Does a remote named `X` exist?\" is not \"is this local work pushed anywhere,\" and the gap is a renamed repository. The operational rule that falls out is to treat the output of a sync scan as a *work queue* rather than a *finding list* — each entry earns a cheap verification before any action, particularly when the proposed action is creative (`gh repo create`) rather than corrective. This mirrors the 2026-09-09 conflict triage, where 36 flagged files reduced to 4, and the same day's repo-visibility audit, where liveness had to be checked by reading inside each repo rather than by trusting a status field.\n\nThe self-clearing divergence errors are a distinct and underappreciated case. Session-start hook errors are generated at a single instant and then persist as text in the session's context for as long as the session runs; by the time an operator reads and investigates one, the underlying condition may have been resolved by a scheduled job, another session, or a subsequent push. Checking the live state first — `0 ahead / 0 behind` — costs seconds and prevents an investigation into a problem that no longer exists. The related instinct, recorded on 2026-09-02 as \"another session may have fixed it,\" applies here in its resolved form: the check was run rather than the assumption made in either direction.\n\nTwo mechanical gotchas round out the session. The parallel fetch loop in the sync script broke on `Linda Projects`, a path containing a space, and had to be handled as a one-off — a standing hazard for any shell loop that word-splits paths and a reason to quote or null-delimit in scripts that walk user directories. Separately, a safety guard blocked a compound Bash command, and splitting it into separate calls succeeded. This is the same guard behaviour that also blocked inline PowerShell via Bash earlier in the day, where the workaround was to write the script to a file and execute it; both are instances of the guard reading a command's *shape* rather than its effect, with the reliable route being to decompose into the simplest form the guard recognises.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts]] — The same day's sync work, where the root cause of the conflict files was fixed on the Windows side.\n- [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] — The 2026-09-09 precedent of a flagged list collapsing under per-item inspection.\n- [[concepts/dev-thin-repo-github-consolidation]] — The `~/DEV` → `erash11/DEV` consolidation that established the current repo topology these checks walk.\n- [[concepts/public-repo-privacy-audit-read-inside]] — The companion discipline of verifying repository state by reading inside rather than trusting a listed status.\n- [[concepts/dcg-guard-false-positives-native-tool-workaround]] — The safety guard that blocked the compound Bash command and the inline PowerShell.\n- [[concepts/handoff-doc-uncommitted-tooling-changes]] — The \"another session may have fixed it\" check, applied here to self-cleared divergence errors.\n- [[connections/name-and-flag-are-not-the-artifact]] — Repo names and status flags over- and under-including relative to the underlying work.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-11.md]] — Sync session (21:41): `/dev-sync` on the Mac against `erash11`; `repo-diverged:global-memory` and `repo-diverged:dotclaude` found already self-cleared at 0 ahead / 0 behind; four folders flagged \"not a repo\" identified as tracked by a parent repo, with the lesson to verify before running `gh repo create`; five apparently-missing repos identified as name mismatches or config repos; nothing pulled because nothing was behind (Windows last synced 9/9, Mac-only work since); `Linda Projects` breaking the parallel fetch loop on its embedded space; a safety guard blocking a compound Bash command, resolved by splitting into separate calls.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Claude Code Environment Maintenance\"\naliases: [doctor diagnostics, plugin cache migration, mcp re-auth, effort setting, github MCP plugin, GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN, gh CLI vs MCP, fable-5 model switch, advisor incompatibility, orphan temp_github folders, github MCP 401 header, OAuth fallback disabled]\ntags: [claude-code, tooling, maintenance, infrastructure]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-06-10.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-04-14.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-03.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-09.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-07-09.md\"\ncreated: 2026-06-10\nupdated: 2026-08-01\n---\n\n# Claude Code Environment Maintenance\n\nPeriodic maintenance of the Claude Code environment surfaces configuration-drift issues that accumulate silently between active sessions. Four notable maintenance passes have been documented: a 2026-06-10 session that resolved plugin cache migration, stale MCP authentications, and temp-directory accumulation; a 2026-06-03 session that diagnosed and resolved a missing `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` for the GitHub MCP plugin; a 2026-06-09 session that switched the default model to Fable 5 and discovered that the advisor model (opus-4-8) is incompatible with Fable 5 requests; and a 2026-07-09 observation of the GitHub MCP plugin failing to reconnect with an HTTP 401 because a configured `Authorization` header disables OAuth fallback. Most issues are caught via `/doctor` and resolved through `/mcp`, `/plugins`, or targeted configuration changes.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- `/doctor` flagged 4 issues on 2026-06-10: three claude.ai MCP servers (Supabase, Vercel, Google Calendar) needing re-authentication, and the `cli-printing-press` plugin not found at its expected versioned cache path.\n- Claude Code migrated its plugin cache from a flat layout (`cache\\<plugin>\\`) to a versioned layout (`cache\\<plugin>\\<plugin>\\<version>\\`), likely due to a Claude Code update. Plugins installed under the old layout fail version checks even though their skills may still work via a fallback path; the fix is to reinstall via `/plugins`.\n- Four stale `temp_github_*` directories were found in the plugins cache, left over from interrupted installs in late May/early June — safe to delete once confirmed unused. Interrupted plugin downloads can both lose a plugin's cache entry and leave orphan `temp_github_*` folders; `/plugin` reinstall + `/reload-plugins` restores the cache, and the orphan folders require manual deletion.\n- For MCP servers not actively used from Claude Code (Supabase, Vercel, Google Calendar), disconnecting them in claude.ai's connector settings permanently silences `/doctor` re-auth warnings and eliminates the tool-schema token overhead of loading unused MCP tool definitions each session.\n- The `compound-engineering` plugin was installed via `/plugin` and activated with `/reload-plugins`, adding `ce-*` skills/agents for planning, code review, commits, and PR workflows. `/effort` was set to \"high\" as the new session default.\n- **GitHub MCP plugin vs. `gh` CLI (2026-06-03):** the `github@claude-plugins-official` MCP plugin requires a `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` environment variable to pass `/doctor`. If all GitHub operations use the `gh` CLI directly (authenticated via `gh auth login`; scopes: gist, read:org, repo, workflow), the MCP plugin is redundant — the cleanest fix is disabling it with `\"github@claude-plugins-official\": false` in `.claude/settings.json`. Alternatively, set `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` from an existing PAT (e.g., reuse `CODEX_GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` if it has matching scopes).\n- **Fable 5 + medium effort + advisor incompatibility (2026-06-09):** default model switched to Fable 5, effort set to medium. The advisor model (opus-4-8) is incompatible with the Fable 5 request model — requests return 400 errors — and was disabled. When switching request models, the advisor model must also be verified for compatibility or disabled; mismatched advisor+request combinations produce API errors that are not immediately obvious as a configuration conflict.\n- **GitHub MCP 401 with Authorization header disables OAuth (2026-07-09):** the `plugin:github:github` MCP server failed to reconnect with HTTP 401 — the server rejected the configured `Authorization` header, and OAuth fallback is disabled whenever `headers.Authorization` is explicitly set. Fix: check/refresh the token, or remove the `headers.Authorization` config entirely to re-enable OAuth negotiation.\n\n## Details\n\nThe `/doctor` command acts as a quick health check across MCP connections, plugin installs, and cache state. On 2026-06-10 it caught a cache-format mismatch that was not the user's fault: a Claude Code update changed how plugins are cached on disk (from a flat directory per plugin to a versioned subdirectory), and `cli-printing-press` had been installed before that change. The plugin's files were technically present, but the version-check logic looked in the new versioned path and didn't find them — the recommended fix was simply to reinstall the plugin via `/plugins` so it lands in the new layout. The MCP re-auth findings (Supabase, Vercel, Google Calendar) were treated as low priority: rather than re-authenticating connectors that may not be exercised from Claude Code at all, the suggested path was to disconnect them at the source (claude.ai connector settings), which both stops the recurring `/doctor` warning and reduces the standing tool-schema footprint in every session.\n\nOn 2026-06-03, a separate `/doctor` run flagged the `github@claude-plugins-official` MCP server as invalid — specifically, no `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` environment variable was set. Investigation confirmed that all GitHub interactions in this environment already flow through the `gh` CLI (authenticated separately in `~/.config/gh/`), making the GitHub MCP plugin effectively unused. The two resolution options are: (1) disable the plugin entirely in `.claude/settings.json` — the simplest fix when the plugin genuinely adds no value; or (2) set `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` using an existing PAT with compatible scopes, avoiding a new token generation. In this case, `CODEX_GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` already existed with sufficient scopes, so `setx GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN <value>` was used. Note: `setx` writes to the Windows registry immediately but requires fully restarting VS Code for the change to propagate to the Claude Code process — see [[concepts/windows-setx-env-propagation]] for the stale-environment gotcha this can trigger.\n\nOn 2026-06-09, a maintenance session switched the default model from Sonnet-tier to Fable 5 and changed effort to medium. The advisor feature, which provides secondary-model review of responses, uses a separately-specified model (opus-4-8 in this configuration). When the request model is Fable 5, the opus-4-8 advisor returns 400 API errors because the two models are incompatible in the advisor/request pairing. The advisor was disabled as a result. The lesson: model switches require verifying the full model configuration stack — not just the primary request model but also any secondary models (advisor, sub-agent defaults, tool-use overrides) that may have incompatible pairings.\n\nOn 2026-07-09, the GitHub MCP plugin surfaced a distinct failure mode from the 2026-06-03 missing-token case: here a token/header *was* configured, but the plugin failed to reconnect with HTTP 401 because the server rejected the supplied `Authorization` header. The non-obvious mechanism is that setting `headers.Authorization` explicitly disables the plugin's OAuth fallback — the plugin assumes a manually-supplied header means the user wants header-based auth exclusively, so it does not fall back to OAuth negotiation when the header is rejected. The result is a hard 401 with no automatic recovery. The two fixes are to refresh the token behind the header, or to remove the `headers.Authorization` config so OAuth negotiation is re-enabled. This reinforces the broader 2026-06-03 finding that the GitHub MCP plugin is often redundant when `gh` CLI already handles all GitHub operations — disabling the plugin outright sidesteps both the missing-token and the header-401 failure modes.\n\nTogether these maintenance passes confirm the same operational pattern: the `/doctor` command's value is proportional to how regularly it is run and how promptly its findings are triaged. Issues like the plugin cache migration, the unused GitHub MCP plugin, the advisor incompatibility, the header-401 reconnect failure, and the orphan temp folders can accumulate silently for weeks without causing visible failures, but each unresolved warning represents either a real reliability risk or unnecessary token overhead that degrades session quality.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/custom-claude-code-skills]] — Skill creation later on the same 2026-06-10 maintenance day depended on plugins and skills registering correctly, which this maintenance pass helped ensure.\n- [[concepts/daily-onramp-automation]] — The on-ramp automation pilot run later on 2026-06-10 operates inside this same Claude Code harness.\n- [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] — An earlier (2026-04-14) setup of the persistent-memory hook system that lives in the same `~/.claude/` configuration this maintenance pass audited.\n- [[concepts/windows-setx-env-propagation]] — The Windows registry env-var propagation gotcha encountered while applying the `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` fix on 2026-06-03.\n- [[concepts/book-advisor-system]] — The `/advisor` invocation on 2026-07-09 that surfaced the GitHub MCP 401 also confirmed the current advisor library state (one persona).\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-06-10.md]] — `/doctor` diagnostics, plugin cache fix, MCP re-auth guidance, `compound-engineering` plugin install, `/effort` default change.\n- [[daily/2026-04-14.md]] — Source of the earlier memory-compiler hook setup referenced above.\n- [[daily/2026-06-03.md]] — GitHub MCP plugin `/doctor` warning, `gh` CLI vs. MCP investigation, `CODEX_GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` reuse via `setx`, and stale VS Code environment diagnosis (~15:00 session).\n- [[daily/2026-06-09.md]] — Fable 5 model switch (14:07): effort set to medium, opus-4-8 advisor disabled due to 400 API incompatibility with Fable 5, orphan `temp_github_*` folders confirmed and deleted.\n- [[daily/2026-07-09.md]] — GitHub MCP 401 reconnect failure (09:35): server rejects configured `Authorization` header, OAuth fallback disabled when `headers.Authorization` is set; fix is token refresh or header removal.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/machine-restore-closeout-handoff.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Machine-Restore Closeout and Handoff Note\"\naliases: [restore closeout, 2026-08-06-restore-closeout, handoff note, global-memory handoffs, restore checklist, parked items, compiler backlog 54 logs, zotero MCP registration]\ntags: [workflow, machine-restore, handoff, second-brain, claude-code, project-management]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-11.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-11\nupdated: 2026-08-11\n---\n\n# Machine-Restore Closeout and Handoff Note\n\nThe 2026-08-11 end-of-day session closed out a multi-day machine-restore checklist: an SSD backup baseline, a Zotero CLI/MCP bundle installation, cleanup of a failed-run artifact, and a written handoff note committed to the global-memory repository. The closeout distinguishes between items genuinely finished, items intentionally left to drain on their own schedule, and items deliberately parked.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Handoff artifact:** `~/.claude/global-memory/handoffs/2026-08-06-restore-closeout.md`, committed and pushed — a durable, versioned record of restore state rather than a memory-tier entry.\n- **Zotero MCP registered user-scope:** binary at `~/go/bin/zotero-research-library-pp-mcp`, registered as `zotero`; bundle at `~/printing-press/library/zotero-research-library/zotero-research-library.mcpb`.\n- **Backup baseline closed:** first full `CRASHBOX` run completed clean (OK, 0 errors, 21:25).\n- **Self-draining items are not blockers.** The compiler backlog of 54 daily logs drains on the nightly schedule; PR monitors for #1667 and #1668 were intentionally left stopped because the ball is on the maintainer's side.\n- **Parked items carried forward unchanged:** `GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD`, Milvus, and skill-tax — the same three items parked during the 2026-08-06 loose-ends sweep, confirming they remain deliberate deferrals rather than forgotten work.\n\n## Details\n\nThe closeout illustrates a three-way classification that keeps an end-of-day review honest. The first class is genuinely complete work with a verifiable artifact: the backup ran clean and produced a receipt, the MCP binary was registered and its bundle staged, and the handoff note was committed and pushed. The second class is work that is neither done nor blocking because a scheduled process will drain it without attention — the 54-log compiler backlog is the canonical example, since the nightly compile job consumes it incrementally, so listing it as an action item would misrepresent it as requiring human effort. The third class is work owned by someone else or explicitly deferred: PRs #1667 and #1668 sit with the maintainer, so the background monitors watching them were stopped rather than left running to generate noise.\n\nThe parked trio — `GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD`, Milvus, and skill-tax — is notable for having been carried unchanged since the 2026-08-06 sweep. Restating them at closeout rather than dropping them preserves the distinction between a deliberate deferral and an item that quietly fell off the list; a parked item that stops appearing in closeouts becomes indistinguishable from forgotten work. Routing the whole picture into a committed handoff file under `~/.claude/global-memory/handoffs/` rather than into a memory tier reflects the same routing discipline documented elsewhere: durable state that already has a natural on-disk home should live there, with memory reserved for pointers and conventions. Stopping the PR monitors also aligns with the finding that background monitor events are unreliable — a monitor left running against work that cannot progress produces duplicate or spurious notifications rather than signal.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/crashbox-ssd-backup-baseline]] — The backup leg of the same closeout; its clean first baseline was one of the three completion criteria.\n- [[concepts/launchd-backup-receipt-race-repo-reconciliation]] — The 2026-08-06 loose-ends sweep that originally parked `GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD`, Milvus, and skill-tax and left repos pending reconciliation.\n- [[concepts/background-monitor-signal-reliability]] — Rationale for leaving the #1667/#1668 monitors stopped rather than running them against maintainer-owned work.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — The content-to-tier routing logic that puts a committed handoff file on disk rather than duplicating restore state into a memory tier.\n- [[concepts/zotero-research-library-pp-cli]] — The Zotero CLI whose MCP binary and `.mcpb` bundle were installed and registered during this closeout.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-11.md]] — Restore-closeout session (18:04): handoff note committed and pushed, Zotero MCP binary registered user-scope, backup baseline completed clean, PR monitors left stopped, compiler backlog and parked items carried forward.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/speedprofiling-velocity-project.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"SpeedProfiling Project (Baylor Football Velocity Profiling)\"\naliases: [SpeedProfiling, velocity profiling, Baylor football speed profiling, Catapult 10Hz pull, catapultR credentials API, cfbfastR wallclock, GAMES config table, ticket-driven implementation, Alex workshop scripts, kickoff surge alignment, on-field sideline separation, 7-ticket plan, feasibility probe gate, token revocation vs file deletion]\ntags: [baylor-athletics, football, sports-science, data-pipeline, catapult, R, keystoneos, velocity-profiling]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-07-18.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-07-20.md\"\ncreated: 2026-07-18\nupdated: 2026-08-07\n---\n\n# SpeedProfiling Project (Baylor Football Velocity Profiling)\n\nSpeedProfiling is a Baylor football velocity-profiling project that pulls full-game 10 Hz Catapult GPS data, aligns it with college football play-by-play (via CFBD / cfbfastR), and produces per-athlete speed/velocity profiles adapted from a workshop method authored by \"Alex.\" The project is developed as a private R repository (`github.com/erash11/SpeedProfiling`, 176-package renv lockfile) executed against a 7-ticket implementation plan tracked in the KeystoneOS `.scratch/speedprofiling/issues/` directory, using the mattpocock `implement` and `code-review` skills. On 2026-07-18, tickets 01, 02, 04, and 07 were advanced; on 2026-07-20, ticket 05 (GPS↔play-by-play merge, on-field separation, workload tables) was closed and shipped on PR #3.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Ticket-driven, per-session workflow:** the project is decomposed into 7 tickets; each ticket is implemented in a fresh session via `/mattpocock-skills:implement <ticket path>`. The `Status:` line in each ticket file is the claim mechanism that prevents parallel-session collisions. Eric prefers fresh parallel sessions per ticket over driving all tickets from one session.\n- **catapultR credentials-object API change:** the installed catapultR uses a new credentials-object API — `ofCloudCreateToken(sToken=…, sRegion='America')`; the old `token=` argument no longer exists. `R/01_get_data.R` was rewritten for this API in ticket 04.\n- **Deleting a token file does not revoke it server-side:** the old 2023 Catapult token had to be revoked manually in the OpenField UI — removing the local JWT file does not invalidate the token on Catapult's servers. Ticket 02 hardened `.gitignore` against `AccessTokens.txt`/`*token*.txt` and migrated live values into `.Renviron`, but server-side revocation of a superseded token is a separate manual step (logged as an Eric-manual action item).\n- **Full 10 Hz pull verified (ticket 04):** the pull retrieved 8,932,931 rows at true 10 Hz (0.1 s spacing, max velocity 20.3 mph) for all 61/61 athletes (79 MB, gitignored) plus 188 play-by-play plays; 69/69 testthat tests pass. A feasibility probe gate hard-stops before the bulk pull unless genuine 10 Hz rows with all required columns return.\n- **Ticket 05 — GPS↔play-by-play alignment (2026-07-20):** merged 10 Hz GPS with play-by-play using the kickoff surge signature as a numerical time anchor, separated on-field from sideline athletes via a two-part rule (sideline boundary + metabolic-power gate), and produced per-play workload tables. Closed on PR #3. See [[concepts/speedprofiling-gps-playbyplay-alignment]].\n- **Game selection is config, not code:** game selection moved from hardcoded `GAME_*` variables to a `GAMES` table in `R/config.R` (activity ID + cfbd args per row). Adding a game is a one-row config edit; an empty `GAMES` renders the activity list to pick from. Per-game `.rds` caching pays the slow pull once.\n- **Stacked PRs merge in order #1 → #2 → #3;** ticket 04's PR #2 is stacked on ticket 03's PR #1.\n\n## Details\n\nThe project adopts a set of workshop R scripts (from an author \"Alex\") as reference material and re-implements them ticket by ticket against Baylor's live data sources. Ticket 01 established the repo, adopted the Drive scripts/docs, and set FERPA rules in AGENTS.md with a CLAUDE.md adapter, gitignore protection, and a private repo. A standards review of the adopted scripts found four real defects — notably a timestamp-conversion bug in `01_get_data.R` and play-end windows in `02_combine_data.R` that erroneously absorb halftime gaps — which were logged to the tracker and mapped to downstream tickets 03–06 rather than patched twice in reference code. Ticket 02 decoded the Catapult JWT scopes (`connect, catapultr, sensor-read-only`, expiry 2126), confirming the sensor scope needed for the 10 Hz feasibility probe, and migrated tokens from an untracked `AccessTokens.txt` (hardened out via `.gitignore` patterns `AccessTokens.txt` and `*token*.txt`) into `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Documents\\.Renviron` (`CATAPULT_TOKEN`, `CFBD_API_KEY`). Two environment notes surfaced during setup: the local `G:\\My Drive` mount is much faster for adopting workshop files than downloading them through the Drive API, and `reactablefmtr` was archived off CRAN so it must be installed from GitHub for a clean renv restore (the renv snapshot-type setting also had to be fixed for `renv::status()` to report clean).\n\nTicket 04 delivered the core game-data pull (8.9M rows at true 10 Hz for 61 athletes), verified end-to-end (69/69 tests, both `.rds` caches hit on re-run). The BYU game (BYU – G5 – 09.28.24) was auto-selected because ticket 02 had already verified its play-by-play and Eric was unavailable to pick interactively; swapping games is a one-row `GAMES` config edit. Ticket 04 anticipated two forward-looking findings for ticket 05: Catapult X/Y coordinates return in venue coordinates requiring a transform, and the cfbfastR `wallclock` column might simplify GPS↔play timestamp alignment. Ticket 05 (2026-07-20) resolved both — the venue-coordinate issue via PCA field-frame recovery and the timestamp alignment via the kickoff surge signature rather than `wallclock`. A recurring environment note: background R tasks can report a false \"exit code 1\" even when the script log shows a clean end-to-end run, so the log must be checked before assuming failure (see [[connections/background-task-exit-code-false-failure]]). Ticket 03 (analysis core + tests) and ticket 07 (KeystoneOS/SecondBrain integration) were also run as parallel fresh sessions, and ticket 06 (velocity profiles — sprint detection, NLS fits, bootstrap CIs, position-group comparisons) remains downstream.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/speedprofiling-gps-playbyplay-alignment]] — The ticket-05 alignment, on-field separation, and workload-table work in detail (kickoff surge signature, PCA field frame, metabolic-power gate).\n- [[concepts/speedprofiling-keystoneos-vault-integration]] — The ticket-07 KeystoneOS registry + SecondBrain vault note integration for this project.\n- [[concepts/keystoneos-project-registry]] — SpeedProfiling was registered into the KeystoneOS project registry (ticket 07); the registry integration and vault notes are the project's cross-system anchoring step.\n- [[concepts/catapult-connect-api-reference]] — The Catapult API reference documenting the token model and venue-coordinate behavior; SpeedProfiling's catapultR credentials-object API change and practice-field venue metadata extend the same integration-friction theme.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-worktree-gotchas]] — Tickets 04 and 05 used stacked worktrees and stacked PRs; the project's workflow relies on the same worktree machinery and merge-order discipline.\n- [[connections/sports-tech-vendor-integration-friction]] — The catapultR API change and venue-coordinate dead end are further instances of Catapult integration requiring empirical discovery rather than documentation assumptions.\n- [[connections/background-task-exit-code-false-failure]] — The false exit-1 notification from ticket 04's background pull generalizes to this project's R task execution.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-07-18.md]] — Ticket 01/02 completion (13:29): FERPA setup, script defects mapped to tickets 03–06, JWT scope decode, `AccessTokens.txt` → `.Renviron` migration, catapultR API change, manual OpenField token revocation (file deletion ≠ revocation), `G:\\My Drive` vs Drive API speed, renv/CRAN gotchas (reactablefmtr archived off CRAN, snapshot-type fix). Ticket 04 (14:09): 10 Hz pull for 61 athletes (8.9M rows), GAMES config table, feasibility probe gate, BYU game auto-selection, stacked worktree/PR, venue-coordinate and wallclock findings for ticket 05, background-task-exit-1 false-failure note.\n- [[daily/2026-07-20.md]] — Ticket 05 review-fix and ship-out session: kickoff surge signature alignment anchor, PCA field-frame recovery, two-part on-field rule, PR #3 out of draft, ticket 06 unblocked.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Stack Doctor Sweep (2026-08-11): Self-Inflicted Repo Divergence and a Lying Dry Run\"\naliases: [claude-stack-doctor sweep, repo-diverged skills, skill-tax false positive, daily-backup.ps1 dry-run bug, MEMORY.md 100 percent, SecondBrain two-writer conflict, flush-error version skew 0.2.136]\ntags: [claude-code, automation, maintenance, git, memory, powershell, gotcha]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-11.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-01\nupdated: 2026-09-01\n---\n\n# Stack Doctor Sweep (2026-08-11): Self-Inflicted Repo Divergence and a Lying Dry Run\n\nA `/claude-stack-doctor` run on 2026-08-11 after a three-day gap took the automation/memory stack from 8 issues to 6 (three of the remaining six being lagging indicators that clear on the next nightly backup). The sweep's most valuable findings were not the fixes themselves but three cases where the *monitoring layer* was the defect: a nightly backup that committed its own generated files and so diverged a repo every night, a `skill-tax` check measuring the wrong quantity, and a `-DryRun` flag that reported git operations it never performed.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **`repo-diverged:skills` was self-inflicted.** Six tracked files were regenerated every run (`last-weekly-run.log` plus five `__pycache__` entries), so the nightly backup committed churn and diverged the repo nightly. Untracking and gitignoring them was the sweep's highest-leverage fix.\n- **`daily-backup.ps1 -DryRun` was lying.** The `Log` calls at lines 28/38 sat *outside* the `if (-not $DryRun)` guards at 24/34, so a dry run printed \"committed / pushed\" for git operations it never ran — briefly fooling the sweep itself. Fixed to `[dry-run] would commit`.\n- **`skill-tax` was a false positive from the monitor's own logic:** it counted all 133 on-disk `SKILL.md` files with no knowledge of `skillOverrides`. Verified empirically first (0 of 72 overridden skills leak into the listing; 61 expected, 60 seen), *then* the script was patched — the two candidate fixes were opposite, so the discriminating check had to precede either.\n- **`flush-error` recurrence across 8/08 and 8/11 indicated version skew, not contention:** `claude-agent-sdk` was 26 versions behind (0.2.110 → 0.2.136); upgrading restored flushes, and the memsearch flush patch was confirmed to survive the package operation.\n- **Both sides preserved on a two-writer conflict.** Four SecondBrain morning briefings differed from committed origin versions; origin's won by default and are live, with local copies staged at `doctor-backup-20260807-135924/secondbrain-local-briefings/*.local.md` — fully reversible rather than adjudicated on the spot.\n\n## Details\n\nThe three monitoring-layer defects share a structure worth naming: each produced a confident, well-formatted signal about a system state it was not actually measuring. The dry-run bug is the purest form — logging placed outside the guard it describes means the flag's entire purpose (previewing without acting) is silently void, and the operator's trust in the preview is inversely related to its accuracy. `skill-tax` is the same failure one level up: the check counted files on disk, a proxy that was correct before `skillOverrides` existed and became wrong afterward, so a previously-completed cleanup appeared not to have landed. The correct response in that case was explicitly *not* to fix either candidate immediately, because the two possible remedies (clean up more skills, or patch the counter) pointed in opposite directions; running the discriminating check first — do overridden skills leak into the listing? — decided which. The `repo-diverged:skills` case is the automation eating its own output: a backup job that commits generated artifacts guarantees divergence on every run, making a real alarm fire nightly for a cause the alarm cannot describe.\n\nThe sweep's restraint is as instructive as its fixes. Several issues were deliberately left alone with reasons recorded: 73 unversioned skill files (a design fork — adding `~/.agents/skills` to `backup-manifest.json` was proposed but not applied), unmanaged `DEV` directories (the user's scope call), a 4-log compiler backlog (self-healing at 2/night against ~1/day, and forcing it costs real money), and `snapshot:sessions` (an rsync race against a live Codex file, not rot). Two items were escalated as genuinely not auto-fixable and left open: Outlook auth expired, so morning briefings were shipping with 0 email threads pending an interactive `reauth.sh` run, and Google Drive 154h stale, with files reaching the local `G:` cache but GoogleDriveFS not syncing. The `MEMORY.md` file was at 100% — the next write would have failed — and was consolidated to 93% by shortening wording while keeping all 16 threads, rather than dropping any. Finally, two `flush-error` session IDs were classified benign only after evidence (the IDs exist nowhere; the DEV slug holds 28 transcripts, 3 in the last 24h), on the explicit principle of not classifying by inference; and two self-corrections were made before reporting (an \"8 → 2\" claim against a raw verdict of 6, and a claim that the /doctor changes were unversioned when the nightly backup had in fact committed them).\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/automation-stack-doctor]] — The recurring health-check routine this sweep instantiates; that article's post-restart ALL CLEAR is the same tool run under different conditions.\n- [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] — The `claude-agent-sdk` version-skew failure mode that recurred here (0.2.110 → 0.2.136), matching the documented `uv`-upgrade remedy.\n- [[concepts/committed-symlink-repo-landmine]] — The `~/.claude/skills` `git pull` that destroyed 73 files on 8/07; this sweep verified 21/21 symlinks resolve and 154 `SKILL.md` reachable after its own pull.\n- [[concepts/skill-description-budget-ceiling]] — The skill-listing budget that `skill-tax` exists to monitor; the false positive was a measurement error against that same ceiling.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — Another case where a backup subsystem's status reporting was narrower or staler than the operational question being asked.\n- [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]] — The general pattern the dry-run and `skill-tax` findings both instantiate.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-11.md]] — Stack doctor sweep (backfilled 2026-08-29 15:02): 8 → 6 verdict, `repo-diverged:skills` traced to six self-generated tracked files, `skill-tax` false positive verified empirically before patching, `daily-backup.ps1 -DryRun` logging outside its guards, `claude-agent-sdk` 26-version skew, `MEMORY.md` 100% → 93% with all 16 threads intact, SecondBrain two-writer conflict resolved by staging both sides, deliberately-deferred items with reasons, two open user-actionable items (Outlook reauth, GoogleDriveFS), and two pre-report self-corrections. Handoff at `~/.claude/global-memory/handoffs/2026-08-11-stack-doctor-sweep.md`.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/vendored-skill-hand-install-pattern.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Vendored Skill Hand-Install Pattern (~/.agents/skills → Claude + Codex)\"\naliases: [hand-install skills, davidondrej skills repo, npx skills add blocked, skill symlink shape, .skill-lock.json missing hand-installed, persistent-localhost, anti-sleep, 58 skills vetted, 151 to 156 skills]\ntags: [claude-code, skills, installation, macos, symlinks, tooling, workflow]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-04.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-04\nupdated: 2026-09-07\n---\n\n# Vendored Skill Hand-Install Pattern (~/.agents/skills → Claude + Codex)\n\nOn 2026-09-04, the `davidondrej/skills` GitHub repository (58 skills) was vetted against the existing environment and five skills were installed by hand after the official `npx skills add` installer was blocked by the auto-mode permission classifier. The session established a working install shape — vendor directory plus two layers of symlink — verified it empirically rather than by assumption, and documented a real cost of the manual route: hand-installed skills are invisible to the upstream lockfile and therefore to `skills update`.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **53 of 58 skills rejected, with reasons by category:** duplicates of already-installed capability (superpowers, session-handoff, CreateSkill, codex plugin, launchd), dependence on tools absent from this machine (bb, cmux, Herdr, Pi, Cursor, Postgres), a paid DeepAPI key requirement (`risky-changes` and all ten `research-and-web` skills), and hardcoding of the author's own name (`file-tree`, `stop-overthinking`, `short`, `remind`).\n- **Five installed, taking the count 151 → 156:** `persistent-localhost` (runs dev servers as launchd jobs, replacing `&`/`nohup`), `anti-sleep` (`caffeinate` for long or overnight runs), and three slash-only skills — `/decisions`, `/before-building`, `/next-decision`.\n- **`global-agent-guardrails` was skipped for a concrete conflict,** not redundancy alone: `dcg` already covers the ground, and the skill's denylist blocks `security find-generic-password`, which the Fireflies key read depends on.\n- **The working install shape:** copy `SKILL.md` plus `scripts/` and `agents/` into `~/.agents/skills/<name>/`, `chmod +x` the scripts, symlink *relatively* into `~/.claude/skills/<name>`, then symlink `~/.codex/skills/<name>` at `~/.claude/skills/<name>` — every Codex entry points at the Claude path, not at `~/.agents/skills` directly.\n- **Hand-installed skills are absent from `~/.agents/.skill-lock.json`,** so `skills update` will not see them. Registering them retroactively is possible by running the official installer behind a `!` prefix to bypass the classifier; it may simply report \"already installed.\"\n- **Both installs were tested, not assumed:** `persistent-localhost` served a real HTTP 200 on port 5199 and stopped cleanly; `anti-sleep` reached `ASSERTIONS=active` and stopped leaving no stray plists.\n\n## Details\n\nThe vetting ratio — 5 accepted out of 58 — is the substantive finding, and the four rejection categories are reusable as a triage rubric for any third-party skill bundle. Duplication is the largest bucket and the cheapest to check, since an existing capability makes the new skill pure listing overhead against the description budget documented in [[concepts/skill-description-budget-ceiling]]. Missing-dependency and paid-key rejections are hard constraints discoverable by reading the skill body. The author-name-hardcoding category is the least obvious and the most instructive: a skill written for personal use may embed its author's identity in prompts or paths, which makes it silently wrong rather than broken when installed elsewhere. The `global-agent-guardrails` rejection is a fifth, sharper category — active interference, where the skill's denylist would have broken an unrelated working integration (the Fireflies keychain read). That is a case where \"we already have coverage\" understates the reason to decline.\n\nThe symlink topology is worth stating precisely because it is not the obvious arrangement. The canonical files live once in `~/.agents/skills/<name>/`; `~/.claude/skills/<name>` is a *relative* symlink into that directory; and `~/.codex/skills/<name>` symlinks to the Claude path rather than to the vendor path. Routing Codex through Claude rather than pointing both harnesses at the vendor directory means the Claude path is the single indirection point — moving or replacing the underlying skill requires updating one link, not two. The insistence on a relative link for the Claude leg matters given this environment's history with committed symlinks: absolute links that encode a home directory are exactly what broke during the Windows→Mac migration described in [[concepts/committed-symlink-repo-landmine]].\n\nThe lockfile gap is the genuine cost of hand-installing and should be recorded wherever the install is documented. `~/.agents/.skill-lock.json` is the manifest `skills update` consults, so a skill installed outside the installer is functionally present but permanently unmanaged — it will never receive an upstream fix. This is the same silent-omission shape as a backup manifest that does not list a folder: every managed item reports success, and the unmanaged item is invisible precisely because nothing knows to look for it. The offered remedy (re-running the official installer behind `!` purely to register the entries) resolves the bookkeeping without repeating the file operations. A final procedural note from the same session: two prior claims recorded in `project_vendored-skill-install.md` were disproven during this work and corrected in Tier A memory, which is the correct disposition for memory that testing contradicts.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/dcg-guard-false-positives-native-tool-workaround]] — The `rm -rf` guard rule that forced the install script into a copy-only shape, and the `>`-in-Python false positive hit during the same session.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-permission-modes]] — The auto-mode classifier that blocked `npx skills add`, and the `!` one-off bypass offered as the lockfile-registration route.\n- [[concepts/skill-description-budget-ceiling]] — Why installing marginal skills is not free: every listed skill consumes the session description budget, making the 53 rejections a direct saving.\n- [[concepts/committed-symlink-repo-landmine]] — Prior symlink trouble in this same skills tree; motivates the relative-link discipline used here.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-meta-skills-bundle]] — The earlier precedent of cherry-picking a subset from a multi-skill bundle rather than accepting an all-or-nothing install.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — Tier A is where the two disproven install claims were corrected.\n- [[connections/silent-omission-defeats-per-stage-success]] — The lockfile gap is an instance: every installed skill works, yet the update path cannot see them.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-04.md]] — Skill review and install session (13:18): all 58 `davidondrej/skills` vetted with four rejection categories, five installed (151 → 156), `global-agent-guardrails` skipped for its `security find-generic-password` denylist conflict, `npx skills add` blocked by the auto-mode classifier, the `~/.agents/skills` → `~/.claude/skills` → `~/.codex/skills` symlink shape, the `dcg` `rm -rf` rule forcing a copy-only script, `.skill-lock.json` invisibility with the `!`-prefixed registration remedy, empirical tests of `persistent-localhost` (HTTP 200 on port 5199) and `anti-sleep` (`ASSERTIONS=active`), and two disproven claims corrected in `project_vendored-skill-install.md`.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Claude Code Three-Tier Memory Architecture\"\naliases: [three-tier memory, Tier A memory, Tier B scratchpad, Stop hook per-turn, memory tiers, claude memory layers, hook firing mechanics, Tier B near cap, project memory files, memsearch, user_career_timeline, memory routing matrix, what not to store in memory]\ntags: [claude-code, memory, automation, hooks, infrastructure, architecture]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-05-19.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-05.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-18.md\"\ncreated: 2026-05-19\nupdated: 2026-08-07\n---\n\n# Claude Code Three-Tier Memory Architecture\n\nThe Claude Code memory system is composed of three independent layers, each operating at a different granularity and owned by a different mechanism. This architecture was articulated on 2026-05-19 during a session that traced exactly when and how each capture mechanism fires. On 2026-06-05, a practical capacity-management routing pattern emerged for when Tier B global memory approaches its character cap. On 2026-06-18, a worked example clarified how to route a specific piece of context across the tiers — and which content should not enter the memory system at all.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Tier A — Native Claude Code auto-memory:** Built into Claude Code itself; managed per-project; no user configuration required. Operates automatically within a project context; its exact retention and synthesis mechanics are not user-visible.\n- **Tier B — Custom global scratchpad via Stop hook:** A user-configured hook on the `Stop` event, which fires after every conversation turn. This provides incremental, per-turn transcript capture to a global scratchpad file (MEMORY.md) — a granularity that neither Tier A nor Tier C provides.\n- **Tier C — claude-memory-compiler:** The [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] system: `SessionEnd` and `PreCompact` hooks fire shell commands at session teardown or auto-compaction, spawning a detached `flush.py` process that appends structured notes to the daily log; at end-of-day these are compiled into knowledge articles.\n- **The agent never sees a \"session ending\" event.** Session end and compaction are harness-level events; it is the Claude Code harness that runs the hook shell commands, not the agent itself.\n- **PreCompact as a safety net:** In a long session with multiple auto-compactions, PreCompact fires before each one, preventing total context loss if the session later crashes before `SessionEnd`.\n- **When Tier B is near cap, route to Tier A project files:** The global MEMORY.md (Tier B) has a character-cap constraint. When it approaches capacity, new durable facts should be saved to Tier A project-specific memory files (e.g., `project_<name>.md`, `user_career_timeline.md`), which are cross-project searchable via memsearch.\n- **Content-to-tier routing matrix (2026-06-18):** file locations and versioning decisions belong in Tier B as an active-thread entry (\"continue this next session\"); durable personal conventions (e.g. \"always save v1 before editing\") belong in Tier A as a feedback rule; and the actual artifact content (slides, scripts) should NOT be duplicated in memory at all because it already lives on disk. Tier C's daily-log capture provides a compressed \"what did I do that day\" summary but is not a substitute for a durable artifact reference.\n\n## Details\n\nThe three tiers are designed to capture context at different time horizons and granularities, and they operate independently rather than hierarchically. Tier A is fully opaque to the user. Tier B's Stop-hook scratchpad is the most granular: because the Stop event fires after every conversation turn, Tier B can capture mid-session context incrementally in near-real-time. Tier C produces the richest synthesized output — compiled knowledge articles from daily logs — but fires only at session boundaries.\n\nThe independence of the three tiers is their key reliability property. A failure in one does not cascade to the others: if a session crashes before `SessionEnd` fires, the Stop-hook's Tier B scratchpad may still contain the session's important turns. The \"agent never sees session ending\" detail is architecturally important: code written inside the agent context cannot detect or react to its own session ending. Teardown logic must be placed in hook shell commands that the harness executes.\n\nThe Tier B near-cap routing pattern (2026-06-05) addresses a practical capacity constraint. The correct response when Tier B is near capacity is not to compress existing entries — which risks discarding information encoded in current phrasing — but to route new durable facts into named Tier A project memory files, which are surfaced by memsearch across all project contexts.\n\nThe 2026-06-18 worked example makes the routing decision concrete and, importantly, identifies content that should stay out of memory entirely. Asked how best to preserve a working session's context (an RTP presentation's file locations, a v1/v2 versioning decision, and the slides/script themselves), the assistant produced a routing matrix rather than dumping everything into one tier: the file paths and versioning state map to Tier B because they describe an active thread to resume next session; a general preference like \"always save v1 before editing\" maps to Tier A as a durable feedback rule; and the actual slide and script content maps to neither — it already exists as files on disk, so copying it into memory would only create a stale duplicate. The distinction matters because the memory system's value is in cheap-to-retrieve pointers and rules, not in re-storing artifacts that a file path already addresses. The same example noted that Tier C's automatic daily-log capture is a lossy summary (\"good for what did I do that day, not great for pick up exactly where I left off\"), reinforcing that a deliberate Tier B pointer is the right tool when the goal is precise session continuity.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] — Detailed installation and configuration guide for Tier C.\n- [[concepts/daily-onramp-automation]] — A downstream consumer of Tier C's compiled daily-log output.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] — Maintenance pass on the same Claude Code environment that houses all three memory tiers.\n- [[concepts/end-of-day-structured-interview]] — Documents the USER.md 1,375-char cap and the Tier A vs. Tier B (durable vs. active) distinction.\n- [[concepts/weekly-review-synthesis-workflow]] — The end-of-week close-out workflow where near-cap Tier B → Tier A routing was first observed in practice.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-05-19.md]] — Three-tier architecture explanation, hook firing order, and \"agent never sees session ending\" clarification (16:53).\n- [[daily/2026-06-05.md]] — Tier B near-cap → Tier A project files routing pattern observed during end-of-week memory housekeeping (18:21).\n- [[daily/2026-06-18.md]] — Content-to-tier routing worked example (01:29): file locations/versioning → Tier B active thread, \"always save v1\" → Tier A feedback rule, actual artifact content → left on disk, not duplicated in memory.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/claude-code-meta-skills-bundle.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Claude Code Meta-Skills Bundle: Selective Skill Installation\"\naliases: [meta-skills.zip, meta-skills plugin, decision-toolkit, fact-checker, process-interviewer, find-skills, frontend-slides, cherry-pick skills from plugin]\ntags: [claude-code, skills, tooling, automation, infrastructure, windows]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-05-20.md\"\ncreated: 2026-05-20\nupdated: 2026-05-20\n---\n\n# Claude Code Meta-Skills Bundle: Selective Skill Installation\n\nOn 2026-05-20, a `meta-skills.zip` plugin bundle sourced from a YouTube demo was reviewed and selectively installed into Claude Code. The bundle contained 13 skills (not the 8 advertised), of which 5 were chosen based on overlap with existing tools and the user's sport-science/development workflow. The session established a key architectural distinction: Claude Code plugins are all-or-nothing installs, but individual skills can be cherry-picked from a bundle by extracting them directly to `~/.claude/skills/` instead of using the plugin wrapper at `~/.claude/plugins/`.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Plugin vs. skills directory:** `~/.claude/plugins/<name>/` installs an entire plugin as an atomic unit; `~/.claude/skills/<skill-name>/` installs individual skills independently. To cherry-pick from a bundle, extract desired skill directories to `~/.claude/skills/` rather than placing the whole bundle under `~/.claude/plugins/`.\n- **No restart required for skills:** skills installed mid-session to `~/.claude/skills/` are picked up immediately; contrast with plugins, which require a full Claude Code restart or `/reload-plugins` before activating.\n- **Mac-generated zip artifacts:** zips from Mac users contain a `__MACOSX/` metadata folder — always skip it during extraction; it has no functional content.\n- **5 selected skills:** `decision-toolkit`, `fact-checker`, `process-interviewer`, `find-skills`, `frontend-slides`.\n- **8 skipped skills with reasons:** `audio-transcriber` (Fireflies MCP already covers transcription), `agent-browser` (Playwright MCP and claude-in-chrome overlap), `openrouter` (requires separate API key, no current use case), `humanizer` (marketing tool, not applicable), `file-organizer` (unacceptable risk of unintended filesystem changes), `deep-research` (requires OpenAI API key), `prompt-master` and `mcp-builder` (no current use case).\n\n## Details\n\nThe bundle was downloaded as `meta-skills.zip` from a YouTube tutorial that described 8 skills; inspection revealed 13 skill directories plus the `__MACOSX/` metadata folder. Each skill was evaluated against two criteria: (1) overlap with already-installed tooling (Fireflies MCP for transcription, Playwright MCP and claude-in-chrome for browser automation), which would make the skill redundant; and (2) dependency on an API key not already in the environment (OpenAI for `deep-research`, a separate OpenRouter key for `openrouter`), which deferred those skills until a concrete use case justified the setup cost. Skills presenting unacceptable risk — `file-organizer` in particular, which makes autonomous changes to the filesystem — were excluded regardless of utility.\n\nThe cherry-pick-to-skills-dir pattern trades the plugin wrapper's convenience (single-command install, version tracking, coordinated uninstall) for the ability to select exactly which skills enter the Claude Code environment. Once extracted, each skill directory functions identically to one installed via `npx skills add` or the printing-press workflow: the skill's `SKILL.md` is read at session start and its slash command becomes available immediately. The immediate mid-session availability of the skills layer (versus the restart requirement for plugins) means selective installs during an active session are a practical, low-friction operation. This install pattern extends the broader Claude Code skill ecosystem covered in [[concepts/claude-code-cli-marketplace-setup]] and complements the custom skills built for the SecondBrain vault in [[concepts/custom-claude-code-skills]].\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-code-cli-marketplace-setup]] — Earlier (2026-05-13) session establishing the printing-press plugin and GSD skill ecosystem; the cherry-pick-to-skills-dir pattern is an alternative to the bulk-install approaches documented there.\n- [[concepts/custom-claude-code-skills]] — Custom skills built directly for the SecondBrain vault; the meta-skills bundle adds pre-built complementary skills to the same `~/.claude/skills/` layer.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] — The broader discipline of auditing and curating the Claude Code tooling environment; the skill-by-skill inclusion/exclusion decisions here follow the same \"unique value vs. overlap\" framework.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-05-20.md]] — Bundle review, relevance-rating against existing tools, and selective extraction session (16:40).\n\n```\n\n### concepts/daily-onramp-automation.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Daily On-Ramp Automation (Lean Mode)\"\naliases: [on-ramp pilot, onramp.sh, morning briefing automation, must-dos, ctx savings, 4.0 MB savings, 214x compression]\ntags: [automation, productivity, workflow, okrs]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-06-10.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-04-14.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-05.md\"\ncreated: 2026-06-10\nupdated: 2026-06-16\n---\n\n# Daily On-Ramp Automation (Lean Mode)\n\nThe Daily On-Ramp is an automated morning-briefing pipeline that gathers raw inputs via a script (`onramp.sh`), distills them into a short \"must-dos\" list, prepends that summary to the day's `morning-briefings/YYYY-MM-DD.md` file, logs an audit row to `onramp-audit.md`, and backs up the result via commit/push. On 2026-06-10 the pipeline was run as a \"lean mode\" pilot; a second documented run occurred on 2026-06-05 using a stripped-down input set (carry-forward items and OKR scoreboard only, with no calendar events or inbox triage yet present for the day).\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **\"Lean mode\"** deliberately skips reading raw calendar/email JSON and relies solely on `onramp.sh`'s compact output, trading completeness for token efficiency.\n- **The 2026-06-10 pilot** prepended three sections to that day's briefing: ① Carry-forward items (none — no unchecked items from the prior day), ② 6 must-dos, ③ all 7 active OKR next-actions framed as the week's \"big rocks\"; existing inbox-triage content was preserved below the new section.\n- **The 2026-06-05 run** used a sparse input set — only carry-forward items and the OKR scoreboard, with no calendar events or inbox triage yet available. Produced 5 must-dos prioritizing overdue carry-forward items before OKR big rocks. Demonstrates graceful degradation when inputs are incomplete.\n- **Token efficiency quantified:** the 2026-06-10 lean run measured **536 tokens kept in-context** versus an estimated 114.5K if raw calendar/email data had been read directly — 100% of that volume avoided, logged in `onramp-audit.md`. By 2026-06-05, cumulative savings were **4.0 MB across 22 days / 105 conversations** (a 214× compression ratio before `/compact`), confirming compounding value over multi-week usage.\n- **Audit trail:** each run appends a row to `onramp-audit.md` (format: date, time, yes/no). The on-ramp produces a valid output even on days with minimal inputs; carry-forward items from prior must-do lists seed it when no new sources are available.\n\n## Details\n\nThe pipeline's value proposition is token efficiency: rather than having the assistant read raw calendar and email exports each morning, a lightweight gather script pre-aggregates the relevant signals into a compact form, and the LLM's job is reduced to distillation and formatting. The 2026-06-10 run quantified this trade-off explicitly (536 tokens vs. ~114.5K), giving a concrete per-run baseline for evaluating whether lean mode is \"good enough\" going forward or whether some days will need a fuller run that reads raw sources. By 2026-06-05, the cumulative record across 22 active days and 105 conversations showed 4.0 MB of raw context kept out of the window — at 214× the compressed size — confirming that the \"gather-then-distill\" architecture pays compounding dividends as usage continues. The pipeline's per-run savings are substantial on their own; their cumulative effect across weeks is an order-of-magnitude improvement in context budget efficiency.\n\nThe 2026-06-05 run demonstrated the pipeline's graceful degradation when inputs are sparse: with no calendar events or inbox triage yet available for the day, the on-ramp ran using only carry-forward items and the OKR scoreboard. This produced a valid 5-item must-do list (Ruth's Perch camera upgrade decision overdue carry-forward, uploading practice-planning docs to unblock FaceComps for OKR 1.2, resuming the FaceComps grill and setting rep bands, specifying the unified coach view and volleyball intro/buy-in for OKR 1.1, and advancing basketball summer initiatives) without requiring the full input set. The sparse-input graceful degradation is a key design property: the on-ramp produces useful output regardless of whether all expected sources are present, rather than failing or outputting nothing on low-input days. The must-dos generated by the 2026-06-10 run were derived from inbox/communications triage done earlier that day (16 needs-reply emails, overdue timecards, stale follow-ups, open scheduling items), making the on-ramp a downstream consumer of that triage output rather than an independent process. The same design principle — pre-process inputs so the LLM only reads a compact summary — was applied two months earlier, on 2026-04-14, when the `coleam00/claude-memory-compiler` hook system was set up to summarize sessions into daily logs automatically.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] — The on-ramp pipeline runs inside the same Claude Code harness that was undergoing maintenance earlier on 2026-06-10; the plugin and skill registrations that maintenance pass verified are dependencies for the on-ramp's execution environment.\n- [[concepts/custom-claude-code-skills]] — Both the on-ramp pilot and the new skills built on 2026-06-10 reflect a broader push toward scripted, token-efficient automation layered on top of Claude Code.\n- [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] — An earlier (2026-04-14) automation pipeline sharing the same design principle: pre-process inputs so the LLM only reads a compact summary, not raw data. The memory compiler produces the daily logs that the on-ramp's OKR scoreboard and carry-forward items draw from.\n- [[concepts/morning-email-briefing-design]] — A related daily-orientation system (designed 2026-05-21) that addresses email and calendar triage in a structured 6-section format; the on-ramp consumes triage output while the briefing system generates it, making the two pipelines complementary at the input/output boundary.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-06-10.md]] — Pilot run details, must-dos list, ctx stats (536 tokens in-context vs. 114.5K raw), audit/backup confirmation, commit \"on-ramp pilot 2026-06-10.\"\n- [[daily/2026-04-14.md]] — Source of the earlier memory-compiler automation sharing the same gather-then-distill design principle.\n- [[daily/2026-06-05.md]] — Second documented run: carry-forward + OKR scoreboard input set only, 5 must-dos generated, cumulative ctx savings quantified (4.0 MB / 22 days / 105 conversations, 214× compression before /compact).\n\n```\n\n### concepts/baylor-tech-inventory-analysis.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Baylor Athletics Technology Inventory Analysis\"\naliases: [Tech_Inventory_Analysis, tech inventory survey, 324 product names, TechInventory_2026-08, Kenny one-page checklist, 33 units inventory, interim read framing, 27 of 33 units, five missing departments]\ntags: [baylor-athletics, deliverables, data-pipeline, reporting, survey, document-generation, onedrive]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-30.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-09-07.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-30\nupdated: 2026-09-07\n---\n\n# Baylor Athletics Technology Inventory Analysis\n\nOn 2026-08-30, the Baylor Athletics Technology Inventory project (`~/DEV/Tech_Inventory_Analysis`) was finalized for distribution: a web report was published, a PDF rendered, a `.docx` added, and all deliverables copied to OneDrive under `00_Primary_Storage_Drive/05_SYSTEMS/SoftwareTechDocs/TechInventory_2026-08/`. The session's most consequential outcome was not a deliverable but a correction — searching meeting transcripts revealed the survey was still open, forcing all four documents to be reframed as an **interim** read rather than a closed finding. A 2026-09-07 verification pass confirmed the data had not moved and surfaced a platform-documentation mismatch that would have produced a false \"no new responses\" answer.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The survey is not closed.** 29 August was Applied Performance's *internal* deadline; the 24 August all-staff meeting set a **department-wide soft deadline of 29 September**. Five non-responding units (27 of 33 in) are outstanding, not delinquent — the original wording read as blame.\n- **As of 2026-09-07 nothing had changed:** 37 response rows, still 27 of 33 units, newest source still `raw/Quick_Inventory_export_2026-08-29.xlsx`, byte-identical by md5 to the OneDrive copy, no newer export anywhere in the home folder. The five outstanding units are Golf (M), Tennis (M), Tennis (W), Volleyball, and Business Office; \"Other\" is a catch-all box, not a real department.\n- **The live survey runs on Microsoft Forms, but project docs point at Google Forms** from the 08-13 proposal era — a trap that would make a `gws` query return a truthful-but-false empty result. See [[concepts/microsoft-forms-vs-google-forms-inventory-mismatch]].\n- **Deliverable format was determined from transcripts, not guessed.** Kenny's own words from the 13 August meeting — \"the more simple and concise, almost like **one page checklist**\" and \"I do think we need to figure out all the systems that we're using\" — plus Jon Allen's \"nobody knows everything that exists\" (80–90% known) support building a one-page executive summary matching the existing `Software_and_Tech_Management_EXEC_SUMMARY_ER_2026-08-14.docx` pattern, with the 10-page report retained as the working document.\n- **PII discipline on publication:** `raw/*.xlsx` contains respondent names and email addresses and was neither published nor copied; the PDF, HTML, and docx were scanned to confirm no PII before distribution. `README.md` (script run instructions) was deliberately withheld, available on request.\n- **Copy, not move, to OneDrive.** Deliverables were placed next to the existing Software and Technology Management framework docs using the established `_ER_<date>` filename convention; local originals were left untouched.\n- **Docx tables are generated from the clean CSVs** (`uv run --with python-docx python3 scripts/build_docx.py`) so prose and data cannot drift — a guard that was needed, since hand-written prose in the same docx said \"roughly thirteen vendors\" while listing twelve (Switch missing).\n- **Data resolutions:** \"Marketing and Fan Experience\" merged into Marketing (34 → 33 units, Marketing no longer a gap); Red Card identified as the athlete meal-money stipend, holding athlete identity/spending data (athlete-data count 59 → 60). Two of 324 product names — **SCRL app** and **JABA** — remain unidentified.\n\n## Details\n\nThe framing error is the session's central lesson and generalizes well beyond this project. The analysis had been written as though the response window had closed, which turns five missing units into an implicit accusation of non-compliance. The actual governance timeline — an internal Applied Performance deadline of 29 August nested inside a department-wide soft deadline of 29 September — was recoverable only from meeting transcripts, not from the survey artifact itself. This is the same discipline applied to the deliverable-format question: rather than infer what Kenny would want from a report, the transcripts were searched for his stated preference, which turned out to be an explicit \"one page checklist.\" In both cases the authoritative record lived in a channel outside the working artifact, and consulting it changed the output materially. The Fireflies transcript corpus functions here as a governance source of truth, the same role it plays in [[concepts/athletics-performance-landing-page-spec]], where a spec was grounded in a specific meeting rather than assembled from assumption. A parallel language edit followed the same source: \"nothing in the current process notices this\" was reworked into forward-looking capability language to match Kenny's known review preferences.\n\nThe 2026-09-07 resume session shows what maintaining an interim deliverable actually costs. The question \"has anything changed?\" was answered by going to the raw `.xlsx` rather than the cleaned CSV, comparing the local and OneDrive copies by md5 rather than by filename, and searching the whole home folder for a newer export — three independent checks to establish a negative. That thoroughness paid for itself by exposing the Microsoft-Forms-versus-Google-Forms documentation drift, which is invisible downstream of the cleaning step and would otherwise have been discovered only after a future session trusted a stale form ID. The practical consequence is that this project's refresh loop is human-gated: the export cannot be pulled from any CLI, so the interim label persists until someone manually downloads a new file and runs `uv run --with openpyxl python3 scripts/clean_inventory.py`, confirming `clean/unmapped_review.csv` is empty.\n\nThe generation-hygiene findings echo failures catalogued elsewhere in this knowledge base. Headless Chrome stamped a `file:///Users/...` path into the PDF footer by default and a page-break rule left a half-empty page — neither of which is detectable from the output's byte size, only from inspecting the rendered PDF, the same render-layer verification rule established in [[connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output]] and exercised on Excel conditional formatting in [[concepts/openpyxl-conditional-formatting-alpha-gotcha]]. The prose/data drift (\"roughly thirteen vendors\" over a list of twelve) is the specific failure that generating tables directly from CSVs is meant to prevent; that the drift survived in the hand-written surrounding prose shows the guard covers only the generated region, and hand-written summary sentences over generated data remain a live drift surface. Because every document rebuilds from the pipeline, the post-29-September plan is mechanical: re-export the survey data, re-run the pipeline, and all four documents regenerate with final numbers — which is also why the one-page executive summary can be built now (marked interim) at negligible cost.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/microsoft-forms-vs-google-forms-inventory-mismatch]] — The 2026-09-07 platform-drift finding, and why the raw `.xlsx` was the only artifact that could expose it.\n- [[concepts/interim-framing-for-open-data-collection]] — The generalized rule extracted from this session's central correction.\n- [[concepts/tier-a-memory-as-the-durable-payload-of-a-verification-session]] — Where the 2026-09-07 finding was stored, and why a verification pass produced memory rather than a handoff.\n- [[concepts/at-utilization-benchmarks-project]] — Another Baylor departmental inventory/metrics deliverable built as a script-regenerable `python-docx` artifact with explicit anonymization rules for outbound documents.\n- [[connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output]] — The rendered-PDF inspection, the re-read that caught the twelve-vs-thirteen vendor drift, and the raw-`.xlsx` check are all ground-truth checks against a plausible-looking intermediate output.\n- [[concepts/athletics-performance-landing-page-spec]] — Also grounds a Baylor deliverable in a specific Fireflies meeting transcript rather than in assumption.\n- [[connections/sports-tech-vendor-integration-friction]] — The inventory enumerates the vendor landscape (324 product names) whose integration friction that article catalogues case by case.\n- [[concepts/baylor-software-tech-doc-set-finalization]] — The sibling document set in the same OneDrive folder, sharing the `_ER_<date>` convention and the exec-summary pattern this deliverable is meant to match.\n- [[concepts/fireflies-meeting-rename-workflow]] — The meeting-transcript corpus consulted here for Kenny's stated preference and the 29 September deadline.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-30.md]] — Tech inventory finalization session (07:08): web/PDF/docx publication and OneDrive copy, transcript-derived interim reframing and 29 September department deadline, Kenny's one-page-checklist preference, PII exclusion of `raw/*.xlsx`, headless-Chrome footer and page-break re-render, vendor-count prose drift, Marketing merge and Red Card resolution, SCRL/JABA open.\n- [[daily/2026-09-07.md]] — Resume and verification session (21:30): direct `.xlsx` check confirming 37 rows / 27 of 33 units with no new export, md5-identical OneDrive copy, five outstanding units named and \"Other\" classified as a catch-all, the Microsoft-Forms-versus-Google-Forms documentation mismatch, CLI-unreachable live responses, and the refresh command with its empty-`unmapped_review.csv` success condition.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/custom-claude-code-skills.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Custom Claude Code Skills: web-scraping, ingest-resource, improve-system\"\naliases: [web-scraping skill, ingest-resource skill, improve-system skill, write-a-skill process, SecondBrain vault]\ntags: [claude-code, skills, automation, second-brain, web-scraping]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-06-10.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-04-21.md\"\ncreated: 2026-06-10\nupdated: 2026-06-15\n---\n\n# Custom Claude Code Skills: web-scraping, ingest-resource, improve-system\n\nThree custom Claude Code skills - `/web-scraping`, `/ingest-resource`, and `/improve-system` - were built in a single session on 2026-06-10 using the standard \"write-a-skill\" process, and registered under `~\\.claude\\skills\\`. The vault-facing skills were tailored to the user's actual SecondBrain vault structure rather than a generic structure inferred from prompt screenshots.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- `/web-scraping` routes by source type: free WebFetch first for static pages, Exa for semantic/discovery search, and Firecrawl v2 for JS-heavy/SPA pages; implemented as stdlib-only Python scripts (`exa_search.py`, `firecrawl_scrape.py`) that compile and fail cleanly when API keys are missing.\n- `/ingest-resource` performs tiered acquisition (WebFetch, then falling back to web-scraping's Firecrawl script) and routes content by \"shelf life\": stable reference material goes to a new `Knowledge/` folder, active-work material goes to the existing `Projects/` folder, and `Notes/` (interview-me territory) and `Inbox/` are left untouched.\n- `/improve-system` offers 5 modes - Audit, Skill Review, Experience, Historical Review, and Foundation - selected via signal-phrase routing; Audit mode archives rather than deletes and reports before touching anything, while Foundation mode hands off to `starter-voice-profile`, `starter-icp-positioning`, and `starter-design-tokens` skills.\n- Both vault-facing skills were adapted to the real SecondBrain vault at `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\`, including its existing frontmatter conventions and wikilink style.\n- Verified API contracts: Exa uses `POST /search` with an `x-api-key` header; Firecrawl v2 uses `POST /v2/scrape` with Bearer auth. Real API keys still need to be added to `~\\.claude\\skills\\web-scraping\\.env` (currently commented placeholders).\n\n## Details\n\nThe three skills were designed to compose with each other rather than operate in isolation: `/ingest-resource` calls `/web-scraping`'s Firecrawl script as a fallback when WebFetch alone can't retrieve a page, and `/improve-system` delegates to the `starter-*` skills for its Foundation mode rather than reimplementing that logic. This composition-first design means improvements to one skill (e.g., a new scraping technique added to `/web-scraping`) can propagate to the others automatically.\n\nNewly written skills register immediately in the available-skills list as long as their frontmatter is valid - the user confirmed this by running `/reload-skills` and `/reload-plugins` after writing all three. The remaining open task is operational rather than design-related: the `.env` file for `/web-scraping` still contains commented placeholder keys for Exa and Firecrawl, and a live smoke test of both APIs was suggested once real keys are added.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] - Skill creation followed the same day's plugin/skill registration maintenance.\n- [[concepts/catapult-connect-api-reference]] - A scraping technique discovered later that day (the ReadMe `.md`-suffix trick) is a candidate addition to `/web-scraping` and `/ingest-resource`'s routing logic.\n- [[connections/web-scraping-skill-and-readme-doc-trick]] - Explores the gap between this skill's routing tiers and the technique that actually worked for Catapult's documentation site.\n- [[connections/obsidian-vault-as-knowledge-hub]] - Connects this session's SecondBrain vault-facing skills to an earlier (2026-04-21) decision to treat an Obsidian vault as the destination for graphify's research-synthesis output from SportScienceResearchRepo.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-06-10.md]] - Skill design, API verification, and registration session.\n- [[daily/2026-04-21.md]] - Source of the earlier (2026-04-21) Obsidian-vault-as-destination decision referenced in [[connections/obsidian-vault-as-knowledge-hub]].\n\n```\n\nThese are the 13 articles (of 285) most related to this log, plus graph neighbours and least-linked articles. This is the relevant context, not a partial view to be cautious about.\n- The Current Wiki Index above lists EVERY article. Consult it before creating a new one, and you may [[wikilink]] any article listed there even if its text is not shown.\n- If this log adds detail, correction, or a new example to an article shown above, UPDATE it: re-emit it at its existing path with the full revised content. Updating existing articles is a primary expected outcome, not a fallback.\n\n## Daily Log to Compile\n\n**File:** 2026-09-22.md\n\n# Daily Log: 2026-09-22\n\n## Sessions\n\n## Memory Maintenance\n\n### Session (06:24)\n\n**Context:** Eric verified the first day of Mac-owned data pulls (GPS, Perch, VALD) after moving them off the Windows laptop, then ran closeout.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- \"Can I turn VALD off on Windows?\" — already off. The disabled step 1 in `refresh_all_data.bat` IS the VALD step, and Windows VALD had been broken since 8/2 anyway.\n- Repeated receipt checks through the afternoon; background watchers reported when each pull finished.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Mac owns all 3 pulls. Schedule: GPS 10:00/16:30, Perch 10:05/16:35, VALD 10:10/16:40.\n- Skip rotating the exposed VALD client secret — repo is private and pulls work. Noted in `_INBOX/docs/STATUS.md`.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- **Mac sleep is the real failure mode for scheduled pulls.** The Mac slept at 3:45 PM. GPS ran on time at 4:32, but Perch and VALD only crept forward during short background wakes and finished ~5:36–5:39 PM. Perch timed out twice before try 3 worked. Same root cause as the 6:30 AM GPS problem.\n- Retries saved the data — all 3 exited 0, data to 9/21 — but the lateness is not reliable.\n- No alerts fired, so the Perch and VALD failure-alert paths are still unproven.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- **Open decision (not set yet):** daily wake with `sudo pmset repeat wakeorpoweron MTWRFSU 16:28:00` vs. leave it. Undo is `sudo pmset repeat cancel`.\n- Test the Perch and VALD failure alerts.\n- Delete the unused GPS database copy on Drive: `FA2026/_Mark_for_Deletion/gps_db_handoff_2026-09-21/`.\n- Handoff: `~/.claude/global-memory/handoffs/2026-09-21-mac-owns-data-pulls.md`. STATUS.md updated in `DataBase_GPS_Reporting`, `PlayerProfiles_FB_03-28-2026`, `ForcePlate_DecisionSystem`, `_INBOX`.\n\n### Session (17:25)\n\n**Context:** Ran `/dev-sync` to bring the Mac in line with GitHub, decided two open GPS items, then ran `/closeout` for 2026-09-22.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Open decision on the 16:28 Mac wake for Perch/VALD pulls → Eric chose Option 2 (leave it). He also confirmed the Windows GPS job is stopped.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- **No `pmset` wake.** Perch and VALD may land up to 1 hour late when the Mac sleeps. Undo/enable command kept in `DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`: `sudo pmset repeat wakeorpoweron MTWRFSU 16:28:00`.\n- Windows GPS writer marked stopped (per Eric's report; not verifiable from the Mac). Two writers = two mismatched databases.\n- Did **not** run `compile.py` — a run costs ~$1.28, so it is Eric's call, not an auto-action.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- Transcript log conflicts between Mac and Windows resolve cleanly with **union merge** — keeps every line from both machines, no `.from-*` files to clean up.\n- A **folder name with a space** (`DEV/Linda Projects`) broke one sync step; had to redo it quoted.\n- The backup manifest does not auto-discover new repos. `TOS_Evidence_Brief` was missing, which made the 09:05 backup report an error. Adding it to `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json` fixed it.\n- Tier A memory does not cross machines — durable GPS decisions were written into the repo's `docs/STATUS.md` plus `global-memory/MEMORY.md` so Windows sees them.\n\n**Sync results (146 repos walked):**\n- Pulled: `_INBOX` (1), `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` (2). Pushed: `CommandCentre` (`server.out`). Cloned: `TOS_Evidence_Brief` (made on Windows 9/21). 22 active, 117 dormant and clean. No conflicts.\n\n**Files changed:**\n- `~/DEV/DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`\n- `~/.claude/global-memory/MEMORY.md`, `transcripts/.errors.log`, `transcripts/2026-09-21.md`, `transcripts/2026-09-22.md`\n- `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json` (backup at `backup-manifest.json.bak-2026-09-22`)\n- `~/DEV/TOS_Evidence_Brief/` (new clone), `graphify-out` (13 files from nightly job)\n\n**Action Items:**\n- **Open:** memory compiler still has 2 backlogged logs; warning stays in `.errors.log` until Eric runs `compile.py` (~$1.28). Command is in `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/README.md`.\n- Revisit the wake decision only if GPS data goes **missing**, not merely late.\n- Carried over from earlier: test Perch/VALD failure alerts; delete `FA2026/_Mark_for_Deletion/gps_db_handoff_2026-09-21/` on Drive.\n\n### Session (17:46)\n\n**Context:** Ran a full Claude Code stack/config audit (install, version, settings, plugins, MCP servers, skills, hooks, permissions) across 50 recent sessions plus a 30-day pass over 303 sessions. User interrupted before any changes were applied.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Audit report delivered: install is healthy — single native copy at `~/.local/bin/claude`, version 2.1.280 (current), all settings files parse, auto mode already default, no agent files, CLAUDE.md files total ~1.9k est. tokens with no duplicates or conflicts.\n- 38 blocked permission actions reviewed; none were plain read-only commands, so no allow rules were proposed.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Proposed (NOT yet applied — user interrupted): disable 5 unused plugins in `~/.claude/settings.json` under `enabledPlugins` — `desktop-commander@synced`, `firecrawl@synced`, `browser-use@synced`, `exa@synced`, `cowork-plugin-management@synced`. Zero uses since install, ~1.9k est. tokens saved per session.\n- Keep `Claude Docs` connector (too new to judge). Keep `persistent-localhost` skill but fix it, not remove it.\n- HyperFrames by HeyGen connector flagged for removal (~400 tokens loaded every session, and it blocks its main tools in Claude Code) — user must do this manually via `/mcp`, per-project.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- A `: \"` sequence inside a YAML `description:` line breaks the whole SKILL.md frontmatter, so Claude Code silently ignores the entire header. File: `~/.agents/skills/persistent-localhost/SKILL.md`. Fix is `description: >-` with the text on following lines. It is a vendored skill, so a vendor update can reintroduce the break.\n- **The dcg hook is dead on Windows** — 913 failures. Windows settings point at the Mac path `/Users/kristinarash/.local/bin/dcg`, so the dcg guard checks nothing there. Related to the existing \"Config encoding damage from Windows\" memory note.\n- The skill-router hook runs on every user message: ~1.6 s typical, 68 s worst case, 2 timeouts recorded.\n- `/context` gives exact live context numbers; audit figures were estimates.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Apply (or explicitly decline) the 5-plugin disable in `~/.claude/settings.json`.\n- Fix the `persistent-localhost` SKILL.md frontmatter.\n- User: run `/mcp` and disable HyperFrames (per-project).\n- Fix the dcg hook path on the Windows laptop.\n- Investigate the slow skill-router hook.\n\n### Session (20:01)\n\n**Context:** PFF_Data_Project — syncing the DuckDB database between Mac and Windows via Parquet snapshots on Google Drive, then fixing a destructive bug in the loader.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- User asked to verify the snapshot load. First verification said \"it worked\" — that was wrong. A deeper check during closeout found the load had silently deleted 12,750 Mac-only rows (2024–2025 `pff_premium` rows in `video_plays`, `team_play`, `quarterback_charting`, `rushing`). The first check only compared 2026, so it missed the loss.\n- Rows were restored from the pre-load backup; a second backup was made first. Verified no other table lost rows.\n- User said \"fix load_snapshot.py first\" before moving on. Fix was written test-first.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- `load_snapshot.py` no longer deletes a whole season before loading. It now merges by primary key: inserts new rows, updates matching rows, leaves local-only rows alone. Rationale: a full-season replace assumes the snapshot is a superset of local data, which is false when each machine has categories the other does not.\n- Dry run now reports two numbers per file: rows to add, and local-only rows kept.\n- Rule recorded to memory: swap the Parquet files on Drive, never the `.db` file itself.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- Verifying only the newest season hides data loss in older seasons. Row-count checks must cover every season the load touches.\n- \"Replace season\" semantics are destructive whenever two machines each hold partial data. Merge-by-PK is the safe default.\n- The load's own pre-load backup (`data/pff_before_load_snapshot_<ts>.db`) is what made recovery possible — keep that behavior.\n- Plain `python3` on the Mac has no `duckdb`; use `~/.local/bin/uv run --with-requirements requirements.txt`.\n- Today's transcript file in `global-memory` conflicted again on pull; resolved by keeping every line from both machines.\n- MEMORY.md hit its 2,500-char cap (was 61 over); had to shorten a line before the write succeeded.\n\n**Files / Artifacts:**\n- PR #4 merged to `main`: https://github.com/erash11/PFF_Data_Project/pull/4\n- Handoff: `~/DEV/Football/PFF_Data_Project/docs/handoffs/2026-09-22-mac-snapshot-load-restore.md`\n- Updated: `docs/STATUS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md` in PFF_Data_Project; `~/.claude/global-memory/MEMORY.md`\n- Backups: `data/pff_before_load_snapshot_20260922_181535.db`, `data/pff_before_restore_premium_20260922.db`\n- Drive snapshot re-exported for 2024 + 2025 (12 files plus full `teams` and `players`); now 6,693,237 rows, +12,750.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- On Windows: `git pull` FIRST (without it Windows runs the old destructive loader), then `python scripts/load_snapshot.py --dry-run`, then `python scripts/load_snapshot.py`. Expect \"adds 12,750\" total on the 2024/2025 `pff_premium` lines.\n- Weekly 2026 pull still pending: `python scripts/pull_season_video_plays.py --season 2026 --refresh`\n- Carried over: memory compiler has 2 backlogged logs; run `compile.py` (~$1.28) to clear the `.errors.log` warning.\n\n\n\n## Your Task\n\nDo NOT use any tools and do NOT attempt to write files — your sandbox cannot\nwrite under this directory. Instead, emit the articles as plain text using the\nexact delimiter format below. The orchestrator parses your output and writes the\nfiles itself.\n\n### Output format\n\nFor each new or updated article, emit a block:\n\n===ARTICLE concepts/<slug>.md===\n<full markdown: complete YAML frontmatter + body, per the AGENTS.md schema>\n===END ARTICLE===\n\nUse `connections/<slug>.md` for connection articles and `qa/<slug>.md` for Q&A.\nSlugs are lowercase and hyphenated. To UPDATE an existing article, emit it with\nits existing path and the full revised content (it overwrites).\n\nAfter the article blocks, emit exactly one index block and one log block:\n\n===INDEX===\n| [[concepts/<slug>]] | one-line summary | 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n===END INDEX===\n\n===LOG===\n## [2026-09-24T19:47:14-05:00] compile | 2026-09-22.md\n- Source: daily/2026-09-22.md\n- Articles created: [[concepts/<slug>]]\n- Articles updated: (none)\n===END LOG===\n\n### Rules\n1. Identify 3-7 distinct concepts worth their own article.\n2. Emit ===NOTHING=== ONLY when the log itself contains no durable knowledge\n   (e.g. \"FLUSH_OK\", or purely transient chatter). \"An article already covers\n   this topic\" is NOT a reason to emit ===NOTHING=== - it is a reason to UPDATE\n   that article with whatever this log adds.\n3. Every article: complete YAML frontmatter (title, sources, created, updated)\n   with `sources` citing 2026-09-22.md; at least 2 [[wikilinks]]; Key Points\n   3-5 bullets; Details 2+ paragraphs; Related Concepts 2+ entries; encyclopedia\n   style — neutral and comprehensive. Follow the AGENTS.md article format exactly.\n4. Create a connection article when the log reveals a non-obvious relationship\n   between 2+ concepts.\n4b. Do NOT re-emit an article whose only change would be the `updated:` date or\n   a restatement of what it already says. Measured on a real run, 4 of 5\n   \"updates\" changed nothing but the timestamp - that is churn: it rewrites\n   files, dirties backups, and invalidates caches while adding no knowledge.\n   Re-emit an article only when you are adding substantive content.\n5. Add one ===INDEX=== table row per article (new or updated).\n\nEmit ONLY the delimiter blocks — no preamble, no commentary.\n"}
