{"parentUuid":"0899c01d-9bcf-4303-8722-11cc0dca1ede","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"session_context","context":{"userEmail":"The user's email address is eric.rash11@gmail.com. Use it only to identify the user, such as for authorship, attribution, or filtering their own work. Never send it to an unrelated service, such as in a request header, URL, or payload, unless the user explicitly asks."}},"type":"attachment","uuid":"19b7b0d8-2c10-432d-8ff5-bbfca209e301","timestamp":"2026-09-25T00:47:16.993Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nAs you answer the user's questions, you can use the following context:\n# userEmail\nThe user's email address is eric.rash11@gmail.com. Use it only to identify the user, such as for authorship, attribution, or filtering their own work. Never send it to an unrelated service, such as in a request header, URL, or payload, unless the user explicitly asks.\n\nIMPORTANT: this context may or may not be relevant to your tasks. You should not respond to this context unless it is highly relevant to your task.\n</system-reminder>"}],"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"efd82c20-18dd-40c4-b3d9-e70a9a007529","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"19b7b0d8-2c10-432d-8ff5-bbfca209e301","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"date","date":"2026-09-24"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"07ca0385-87de-4eca-979d-f661d0d138ea","timestamp":"2026-09-25T00:47:16.993Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nToday's date is 2026-09-24.\n</system-reminder>"}],"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"efd82c20-18dd-40c4-b3d9-e70a9a007529","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"type":"last-prompt","lastPrompt":"You are a knowledge compiler. Your job is to read a daily conversation log and extract knowledge into structured wiki articles.  ## Schema (AGENTS.md)  # AGENTS.md - Personal Knowledge Base Schema  >…","leafUuid":"07ca0385-87de-4eca-979d-f661d0d138ea","sessionId":"efd82c20-18dd-40c4-b3d9-e70a9a007529"}
{"type":"atis-latch","atis":"","sessionId":"efd82c20-18dd-40c4-b3d9-e70a9a007529"}
{"parentUuid":"07ca0385-87de-4eca-979d-f661d0d138ea","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfPEdnh5kWrdcmPkwL1Zc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"===ARTICLE concepts/mac-owned-data-pulls-sleep-lateness.md===\n---\ntitle: \"Mac-Owned GPS/Perch/VALD Pulls and Sleep-Induced Lateness\"\naliases: [Mac owns the pulls, refresh_all_data.bat disabled VALD step, pmset repeat wakeorpoweron, scheduled pull late not missing, GPS 10:00 16:30, Perch 10:05 16:35, VALD 10:10 16:40, two writers two databases]\ntags: [baylor-athletics, automation, macos, scheduling, data-pipeline, catapult, vald, perch, migration]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-22.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-22\nupdated: 2026-09-24\n---\n\n# Mac-Owned GPS/Perch/VALD Pulls and Sleep-Induced Lateness\n\nOn 2026-09-22 the three Baylor athlete-data pulls — GPS (Catapult), Perch, and VALD — completed their first full day running from the Mac after being moved off the Windows laptop. All three exited 0 and carried data through 9/21, but two of the three finished roughly an hour late because the Mac was asleep at their scheduled time. The session's decision was to accept the lateness rather than force a wake, on the grounds that the data arrived and only its arrival time was unreliable.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Schedule:** GPS 10:00 / 16:30, Perch 10:05 / 16:35, VALD 10:10 / 16:40 — all three now Mac-owned.\n- **Sleep, not the scripts, is the failure mode.** The Mac slept at 3:45 PM; GPS ran on time at 16:32, but Perch and VALD only crept forward during short background wakes and did not finish until ~17:36–17:39. Perch timed out twice before try 3 succeeded. This is the same root cause as the earlier 6:30 AM GPS problem.\n- **Retries saved the run but do not make it reliable** — the correct framing is \"late, not missing,\" and the revisit trigger was set accordingly: reconsider only if GPS data goes *missing*, not merely late.\n- **The decision was to leave sleep alone (Option 2).** The enable/undo commands are recorded in `DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`: `sudo pmset repeat wakeorpoweron MTWRFSU 16:28:00` to enable, `sudo pmset repeat cancel` to undo.\n- **Windows VALD was already off.** The disabled step 1 in `refresh_all_data.bat` *is* the VALD step, and the Windows VALD pull had been broken since 8/2 — so the migration inherited a dead leg rather than duplicating a working one.\n- **No alerts fired, so the Perch and VALD failure-alert paths remain unproven** — a successful-but-late run exercises neither the alert nor the absence of one.\n- **Two writers would mean two mismatched databases**, so the Windows GPS writer was marked stopped (per Eric's report; not verifiable from the Mac).\n- **The exposed VALD client secret was deliberately not rotated** — the repo is private and the pulls work; the deferral is recorded in `_INBOX/docs/STATUS.md`.\n\n## Details\n\nThe substantive insight is that a scheduled job on a laptop has an execution precondition the scheduler does not enforce: the machine must be awake. macOS will grant short background wakes that let a long-running pull inch forward without ever giving it a contiguous window, which is why Perch timed out twice and both late jobs finished in a ragged ~17:36–17:39 cluster rather than at a clean retry boundary. From the outside, every observable signal is healthy — exit code 0, data current to 9/21, retries doing their job — and only the timestamp reveals that the schedule is not actually being honored. This is the same shape as the `Ready`-forever triggerless task in [[concepts/windows-backup-task-trigger-gotchas]] and the sleeping-laptop-misses-fixed-time-trigger gotcha recorded there: the scheduling layer reports truthfully about its own narrow predicate while the operational question (did this run when it was supposed to?) goes unanswered. The generalized version is catalogued in [[connections/the-execution-context-silently-edits-the-work-set]] — session lifetime, mount state, and now sleep state all shrink or delay what a run attempts without producing an error.\n\nThe decision to leave `pmset` alone is worth recording as a decision rather than an omission, because it comes with an explicit revisit trigger. Forcing a daily wake at 16:28 would make the afternoon pulls punctual at the cost of waking the machine every day for a job whose output is consumed the following morning anyway; lateness inside a single day is therefore not a defect in the consuming workflow. The condition that *would* justify the wake — data going missing rather than arriving late — is narrower and falsifiable, which keeps the deferral from decaying into an unexamined default. The undo command being stored alongside the enable command in the repo's own `STATUS.md` is the mechanism that makes the choice cheap to reverse.\n\nTwo ancillary findings deserve attention. First, the failure-alert paths for Perch and VALD have still never been exercised: a run that is late but ultimately successful produces no alert and therefore proves nothing about whether an alert would fire on a genuine failure. That leaves the alerting path in the same unverified state described in [[connections/alerting-path-must-not-share-the-monitored-systems-budget]] — untested alerting is indistinguishable from broken alerting. Second, the single-writer rule for the GPS database is a correctness constraint, not a tidiness preference: two machines writing the same database produce two divergent copies, and the Windows job being stopped is asserted rather than verified because the Mac has no way to inspect Windows' scheduler. The unused GPS database copy at `FA2026/_Mark_for_Deletion/gps_db_handoff_2026-09-21/` on Drive was queued for deletion for the same reason — a spare copy of a database is a future source of confusion about which one is authoritative.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/windows-backup-task-trigger-gotchas]] — The Windows counterpart, including the sleeping-laptop-misses-fixed-time-trigger gotcha that recurs here on macOS.\n- [[concepts/windows-backup-recovery-inventory]] — The broader Windows→Mac automation migration this pull handover is part of.\n- [[connections/the-execution-context-silently-edits-the-work-set]] — Sleep state joins session lifetime and mount state as a context fact that silently changes what a run accomplishes.\n- [[connections/alerting-path-must-not-share-the-monitored-systems-budget]] — Why the untested Perch/VALD alert paths cannot be assumed working.\n- [[connections/every-layer-reported-healthy-except-the-destination]] — Exit code 0 and current data are both truthful while the schedule is not being honored.\n- [[concepts/catapult-connect-api-reference]] — The GPS pull's upstream vendor API.\n- [[concepts/perch-api-integration-reference]] — The Perch pull's upstream vendor API.\n- [[concepts/fix-from-the-working-machine-handoff]] — The cross-machine repair pattern; here the durable decisions had to be written where the other machine could see them.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-22.md]] — Memory maintenance session (06:24): first Mac-owned day verified, Windows VALD confirmed already off via the disabled step 1 in `refresh_all_data.bat` (broken since 8/2), VALD secret rotation skipped and noted in `_INBOX/docs/STATUS.md`, Mac sleeping at 3:45 PM causing Perch/VALD to finish ~17:36–17:39 with two Perch timeouts, all three exiting 0 with data to 9/21, unproven Perch/VALD alert paths, and the open `pmset` decision with its undo command. Sync/closeout session (17:25): Option 2 chosen (no `pmset` wake), Windows GPS writer marked stopped with the two-writers-two-databases rationale, revisit trigger set at *missing* rather than late.\n\n===END ARTICLE===\n\n===ARTICLE concepts/duckdb-snapshot-merge-by-primary-key.md===\n---\ntitle: \"Destructive Season-Replace Loader Fixed to Merge-by-Primary-Key (PFF_Data_Project)\"\naliases: [load_snapshot.py, 12750 rows silently deleted, replace season destructive, merge by PK, pff_premium 2024 2025, swap the Parquet not the .db, pff_before_load_snapshot backup, dry run adds and keeps]\ntags: [data-pipeline, duckdb, sync, data-integrity, football, verification, gotcha]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-22.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-22\nupdated: 2026-09-24\n---\n\n# Destructive Season-Replace Loader Fixed to Merge-by-Primary-Key (PFF_Data_Project)\n\nOn 2026-09-22 the PFF_Data_Project's `load_snapshot.py` — which syncs a DuckDB database between Mac and Windows via Parquet snapshots on Google Drive — was found to have silently deleted 12,750 Mac-only rows during a load. The rows were 2024–2025 `pff_premium` records across `video_plays`, `team_play`, `quarterback_charting`, and `rushing`. They were restored from the loader's own pre-load backup, and the loader was rewritten test-first to merge by primary key instead of replacing whole seasons. PR #4 merged to `main`.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The first verification said \"it worked\" and was wrong.** It compared only the 2026 season, so a loss confined to 2024–2025 was invisible; the loss surfaced later during closeout under a deeper check.\n- **\"Replace season\" semantics are destructive whenever two machines each hold partial data.** A full-season delete-then-load assumes the snapshot is a superset of local data — false when each machine holds categories the other does not (here, Mac-only `pff_premium` rows).\n- **Merge-by-PK is the safe default:** insert new rows, update matching rows, leave local-only rows alone. The dry run now reports two numbers per file — rows to add, and local-only rows kept — so the preserved set is visible before committing.\n- **The loader's own pre-load backup made recovery possible** (`data/pff_before_load_snapshot_<ts>.db`); a second backup was taken before the restore, and every other table was checked for row loss.\n- **Row-count verification must cover every season the load touches**, not just the newest one.\n- **Rule recorded to memory: swap the Parquet files on Drive, never the `.db` file itself.**\n- **Pull order matters on the other machine:** Windows must `git pull` *first* — without it, Windows runs the old destructive loader against the corrected snapshot.\n\n## Details\n\nThe verification failure is the more transferable lesson of the two. A row-count check is only as wide as the partition it samples, and checking the season under active work is the natural, cheap, and wrong choice: the current season is the one most likely to be correct, because it is the one the snapshot was built to carry. The damage lived in the historical seasons precisely because nobody was looking there and because that is where the two machines' holdings had diverged over time. This is the same shape as the false-positive verifications catalogued in [[connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output]] and the narrow-predicate reporting in [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]] — the check answered a question (\"is 2026 intact?\") strictly narrower than the one being asked (\"did this load lose anything?\"), and answered it accurately. The corrective rule is stated in scope terms rather than as a one-off fix: verification must span every partition the operation writes.\n\nThe semantic bug underneath is a distribution assumption baked into a verb. `DELETE WHERE season = X` followed by an insert is correct exactly when the incoming snapshot is a superset of what is already there — a valid assumption for a single-writer pipeline, and invalid the moment a second machine accumulates rows the first has never seen. Once two machines each hold partial data, the only non-lossy verb is merge, and merge requires a primary key to decide insert-versus-update. This mirrors the finding in [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]]: revisions supersede, but accumulating records diverge, and the correct verb for a diverging set is merge or append rather than replace. It is also the same trade-off that drove `/MIR` → `/E` in [[concepts/windows-backup-recovery-inventory]] — when the artifact's value is that it holds something the other side may no longer have, \"make identical\" is never the right operation.\n\nThe recovery path is worth preserving as a design endorsement rather than an incident detail. The loader wrote its own pre-load `.db` backup as a matter of course, and that unglamorous habit is the only reason 12,750 rows were recoverable rather than merely mourned; the session explicitly recorded \"keep that behavior.\" Taking a second backup before the restore extended the same discipline to the repair itself — consistent with the tarball-before-destructive-git rule in [[concepts/pre-destructive-git-backup-discipline]] and the caution in [[connections/the-preservation-step-must-itself-be-verified]] that a safety net nobody checks is worse than none. Two environment notes round out the session: plain `python3` on the Mac has no `duckdb`, so the loader must run under `~/.local/bin/uv run --with-requirements requirements.txt`; and `MEMORY.md` hit its 2,500-character cap (61 over) so a line had to be shortened before the rule could be written. After restoration the Drive snapshot was re-exported for 2024 and 2025 (12 files plus full `teams` and `players`), reaching 6,693,237 rows, +12,750.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]] — The accumulating-versus-superseding distinction that makes \"replace season\" unsafe across two writers.\n- [[connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output]] — The first verification's \"it worked\" was an intermediate-layer claim that ground truth contradicted.\n- [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]] — The 2026-only check reported accurately on a narrower predicate than the operational question.\n- [[concepts/pre-destructive-git-backup-discipline]] — The same snapshot-before-the-destructive-step discipline that made recovery possible here.\n- [[connections/the-preservation-step-must-itself-be-verified]] — Why the pre-load backup's continued existence is a design requirement, not an incidental nicety.\n- [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] — The prior case where a \"newer wins\" rule would have discarded 324 unique accumulated rows.\n- [[concepts/memory-cap-relief-by-migration-not-deletion]] — The `MEMORY.md` 2,500-char cap that had to be relieved before the new rule could be written.\n- [[concepts/windows-backup-recovery-inventory]] — The `/MIR` → `/E` decision resolving the same mirror-versus-preserve trade-off.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-22.md]] — PFF_Data_Project session (20:01): first verification wrongly reporting success by comparing only 2026, discovery during closeout of 12,750 deleted Mac-only 2024–2025 `pff_premium` rows across four tables, restore from `data/pff_before_load_snapshot_20260922_181535.db` after taking `data/pff_before_restore_premium_20260922.db`, test-first rewrite of `load_snapshot.py` to merge by primary key with a two-number dry run, PR #4 merged, the swap-the-Parquet-not-the-`.db` rule, the `uv run --with-requirements` invocation, the `MEMORY.md` cap collision, the Windows `git pull`-first ordering, and the re-exported 2024/2025 snapshot at 6,693,237 rows.\n\n===END ARTICLE===\n\n===ARTICLE concepts/claude-code-stack-config-audit-2026-09-22.md===\n---\ntitle: \"Claude Code Stack/Config Audit (2026-09-22)\"\naliases: [50-session audit, 303 sessions 30 days, five unused plugins, HyperFrames connector removal, skill-router hook 68s, dcg dead on Windows 913 failures, YAML colon-quote frontmatter break, context vs estimates]\ntags: [claude-code, maintenance, audit, plugins, hooks, skills, mcp, windows]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-22.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-22\nupdated: 2026-09-24\n---\n\n# Claude Code Stack/Config Audit (2026-09-22)\n\nA full Claude Code stack and configuration audit — install, version, settings, plugins, MCP servers, skills, hooks, and permissions — was run on 2026-09-22 across 50 recent sessions plus a 30-day pass over 303 sessions. The install was healthy: a single native copy at `~/.local/bin/claude`, version 2.1.280 (current), all settings files parsing, auto mode already default, no agent files, and CLAUDE.md files totalling ~1.9k estimated tokens with no duplicates or conflicts. The user interrupted before any proposed change was applied, so the audit's output is a reviewed change list rather than a completed remediation.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Five plugins proposed for disabling** in `~/.claude/settings.json` under `enabledPlugins` — `desktop-commander@synced`, `firecrawl@synced`, `browser-use@synced`, `exa@synced`, `cowork-plugin-management@synced` — all with zero uses since install, for ~1.9k estimated tokens saved per session. Proposed, not applied.\n- **A `: \"` sequence inside a YAML `description:` line breaks the entire SKILL.md frontmatter**, so Claude Code silently ignores the whole header. Hit on `~/.agents/skills/persistent-localhost/SKILL.md`; fix is `description: >-` with the text on following lines. Because the skill is vendored, a vendor update can reintroduce the break.\n- **The dcg hook is dead on Windows — 913 failures.** Windows settings point at the Mac path `/Users/kristinarash/.local/bin/dcg`, so the dcg guard checks nothing on that machine.\n- **The skill-router hook runs on every user message:** ~1.6 s typical, 68 s worst case, with 2 recorded timeouts — flagged for investigation.\n- **38 blocked permission actions were reviewed and none were plain read-only commands**, so no allow rules were proposed — a negative result that is itself the finding.\n- **Judgment calls, not blanket removal:** keep the `Claude Docs` connector (too new to judge), keep `persistent-localhost` but fix it rather than remove it, and flag the HyperFrames by HeyGen connector for removal (~400 tokens loaded every session, and it blocks its own main tools in Claude Code) — which the user must do manually via `/mcp`, per-project.\n- **`/context` gives exact live numbers; the audit's figures were estimates** — a stated limitation of the audit method rather than a measurement.\n\n## Details\n\nThe Windows dcg finding is the audit's most consequential result and generalizes beyond this environment. A safety guard whose hook path points at a nonexistent executable does not announce that it is absent; it fails 913 times and the guarded machine proceeds unguarded. The path in question is a Mac path (`/Users/kristinarash/.local/bin/dcg`) sitting in Windows settings — the same Mac-configuration-flows-back-onto-Windows direction documented in [[concepts/windows-backup-recovery-inventory]], where the Windows backup manifest turned out to be the Mac's. The session connected it to an existing \"config encoding damage from Windows\" memory note, placing it in the same family as the sync-mangling described in [[concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts]]. The structural point is that a guard which fails open is worse than no guard, because the operator's mental model still includes protection: this is [[connections/preconditions-that-check-the-wrong-thing]] applied to a cross-platform path rather than to a swallowed stderr.\n\nThe YAML frontmatter break is a smaller but sharply-shaped gotcha. A `: \"` sequence inside an unquoted `description:` value makes the line ambiguous to the YAML parser, and the failure is not scoped to that key — the *entire* header is discarded, so the skill silently vanishes from the routing surface rather than appearing with a mangled description. The remedy (`description: >-` with the text on following lines) is the standard block-scalar escape hatch. Two second-order observations matter: this is a silent drop, joining the over-650-character descriptions in [[concepts/skill-description-budget-ceiling]] as a second mechanism by which a working skill becomes invisible to routing; and because `persistent-localhost` is vendored — hand-installed per [[concepts/vendored-skill-hand-install-pattern]] — an upstream update can reintroduce the break, so the fix is not permanent.\n\nThe audit's restraint is as informative as its findings. The five-plugin disable was left as a proposal rather than applied; the HyperFrames removal was routed to the user because it is a per-project `/mcp` action; the `Claude Docs` connector was explicitly kept on \"too new to judge\" grounds rather than swept up with the unused ones; and `persistent-localhost` was marked fix-don't-remove. The 38 blocked permission actions producing zero allow-rule proposals is the clearest instance — reviewing them and concluding that none qualified is a result worth recording, since an unexamined block list invites a reflexive broad allowlist. The slow skill-router hook, at ~1.6 s per user message with a 68 s worst case and two timeouts, is the one item with an unquantified cost: it is a per-message tax on every session, and the two timeouts indicate the tail is not merely slow but occasionally failing.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] — The recurring `/doctor`-style maintenance discipline this audit extends to hooks, permissions, and session history.\n- [[concepts/skill-description-budget-ceiling]] — The other mechanism by which a working skill is silently dropped from the session listing.\n- [[concepts/vendored-skill-hand-install-pattern]] — Why the `persistent-localhost` fix is impermanent: hand-installed vendored skills can be overwritten by an upstream update.\n- [[concepts/dcg-guard-false-positives-native-tool-workaround]] — The guard whose Windows installation this audit found dead.\n- [[concepts/windows-backup-recovery-inventory]] — The same Mac-paths-in-Windows-settings failure, in the backup subsystem.\n- [[connections/preconditions-that-check-the-wrong-thing]] — A guard that reports nothing while protecting nothing.\n- [[concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11]] — The prior sweep whose monitoring-layer defects this audit's findings rhyme with.\n- [[concepts/session-context-footprint-audit]] — The earlier always-loaded-context measurement; this audit's ~1.9k-token plugin saving and ~400-token connector cost are the same budget.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-22.md]] — Stack/config audit session (17:46): healthy install at `~/.local/bin/claude` v2.1.280 with clean settings and ~1.9k-token CLAUDE.md total, 50 recent sessions plus a 303-session 30-day pass, five zero-use plugins proposed for disabling (unapplied, user interrupted), `Claude Docs` kept and HyperFrames flagged for manual per-project `/mcp` removal, `persistent-localhost` SKILL.md frontmatter broken by a `: \"` sequence with the `description: >-` fix and vendor-reintroduction risk, dcg hook dead on Windows with 913 failures from a Mac path, skill-router hook at ~1.6 s typical / 68 s worst case with 2 timeouts, 38 blocked permission actions yielding no allow rules, and the `/context`-versus-estimates caveat.\n\n===END ARTICLE===\n\n===ARTICLE concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge.md===\n---\ntitle: \"DEV Sync 2026-09-22: Union-Merge Transcripts, Manifest Non-Discovery, and Cross-Machine Memory\"\naliases: [union merge transcripts, TOS_Evidence_Brief missing from manifest, 146 repos walked, Linda Projects space breaks sync step, Tier A does not cross machines, backup-manifest.json.bak-2026-09-22, 22 active 117 dormant]\ntags: [git, sync, backup, macos, memory, workflow, verification]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-22.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-22\nupdated: 2026-09-24\n---\n\n# DEV Sync 2026-09-22: Union-Merge Transcripts, Manifest Non-Discovery, and Cross-Machine Memory\n\nA `/dev-sync` run on 2026-09-22 walked 146 repositories to bring the Mac in line with GitHub: two pulled (`_INBOX`, `DataBase_GPS_Reporting`), one pushed (`CommandCentre`), one cloned (`TOS_Evidence_Brief`, created on Windows 9/21), with 22 active and 117 dormant-and-clean, and no conflicts. Its durable findings concern how recurring cross-machine friction is resolved: a merge strategy for append-only logs, a manifest that does not discover its own new inputs, and the fact that Tier A memory does not travel between machines.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Union merge is the right strategy for append-only transcript logs.** Mac/Windows conflicts in `global-memory/transcripts/*.md` resolve cleanly by keeping every line from both machines, producing no `.from-*` files to clean up afterward.\n- **The backup manifest does not auto-discover new repos.** `TOS_Evidence_Brief` — created on Windows the previous day — was absent from `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json`, which made the 09:05 backup report an error. Adding it fixed the report (backup of the prior file at `backup-manifest.json.bak-2026-09-22`).\n- **Tier A memory does not cross machines.** Durable GPS decisions were therefore written into the repo's own `docs/STATUS.md` *and* `global-memory/MEMORY.md` so the Windows machine would see them.\n- **A folder name containing a space (`DEV/Linda Projects`) broke a sync step again** and had to be redone with the path quoted — a recurrence of the same hazard seen on 2026-09-11.\n- **`compile.py` was deliberately not run.** A compile costs ~$1.28, so clearing the 2-log memory-compiler backlog was left as the user's call rather than taken as an auto-action; the warning persists in `.errors.log` until then.\n\n## Details\n\nThe union-merge finding names a resolution strategy that fits the artifact's semantics rather than a generic \"take one side\" rule. A transcript log is append-only and each machine appends independently, so the two versions are not competing revisions of the same content — they are disjoint additions to a shared record. Under that structure the correct merge keeps every line, and the usual conflict-file machinery (`.from-*` copies awaiting manual adjudication) is pure overhead because there is nothing to adjudicate. This is the same classify-by-kind-before-resolving discipline applied in [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]], where treating accumulating records as revisions would have discarded 324 unique forecast-log rows, and the same accumulate-versus-supersede distinction generalized in [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]]. Notably, the same day's PFF loader bug was the identical error one layer down — a replace where a merge was required.\n\nThe manifest gap is a recurrence rather than a novelty, and its recurrence is the point. A repo created on one machine does not announce itself to a backup manifest maintained on another; the manifest's coverage is declared, not discovered, so a new repo is invisible to it until a human adds a line. The 09:05 backup error was in this case an honest signal — the manifest check did fire — but the general hazard is the one catalogued in [[connections/silent-omission-defeats-per-stage-success]] and [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]]: every managed item succeeds while the unmanaged item is never counted. Prior instances at [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] and [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] make this at least the third occurrence, which by the reasoning in [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] is the threshold at which the fix belongs in the discovery mechanism rather than in another manual manifest edit.\n\nThe Tier A observation is a routing constraint with immediate practical force. Tier A memory is local to the machine that wrote it, so any decision that a second machine must honor — here, that the Mac owns the pulls and the Windows GPS writer is stopped — cannot live there alone. Writing it into the repository's `docs/STATUS.md` puts it in a location git already replicates, and mirroring it into `global-memory/MEMORY.md` reaches the synced memory surface; between them the decision arrives on the other machine by two independent paths. This extends the content-to-tier routing matrix in [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] with a cross-machine axis, and matches the working-machine-authors-the-fix pattern in [[concepts/fix-from-the-working-machine-handoff]]. Finally, the deliberate refusal to run `compile.py` is a recorded non-action rather than an oversight: a ~$1.28 spend is a user decision, so the backlog warning was left standing in `.errors.log` with the command documented in the compiler's README — consistent with treating `.errors.log` as an escalation channel that clears only on a verified clean run.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] — The prior conflict triage that established classifying by kind before resolving; union merge is the resolution for the accumulating-record kind.\n- [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]] — Why an append-only log must be merged rather than overwritten.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] — An earlier instance of the same declared-not-discovered manifest gap.\n- [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] — The fail-closed manifest guard designed against exactly this omission.\n- [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]] — The general shape: an unlisted item cannot fail a check it was never part of.\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]] — The 2026-09-11 sync where `Linda Projects`' embedded space first broke the parallel fetch loop.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — The tier-routing matrix this session extends with a cross-machine constraint.\n- [[concepts/compiler-backlog-hook-notice]] — The backlog warning deliberately left in `.errors.log`.\n- [[concepts/clear-the-error-log-only-after-a-clean-run]] — Why the warning stays until a verified clean compile, not until the fix is merely intended.\n- [[concepts/mac-owned-data-pulls-sleep-lateness]] — The GPS decisions whose cross-machine visibility motivated the dual write.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-22.md]] — Sync and closeout session (17:25): `/dev-sync` walking 146 repos with `_INBOX` and `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` pulled, `CommandCentre` pushed, `TOS_Evidence_Brief` cloned, 22 active / 117 dormant / no conflicts; union merge resolving Mac/Windows transcript-log conflicts without `.from-*` files; `TOS_Evidence_Brief` missing from `backup-manifest.json` causing the 09:05 backup error, fixed with a `.bak-2026-09-22` backup; `DEV/Linda Projects` space breaking a sync step; Tier A memory not crossing machines, resolved by writing to `docs/STATUS.md` plus `global-memory/MEMORY.md`; `compile.py` (~$1.28) deliberately left as the user's call.\n\n===END ARTICLE===\n\n===ARTICLE connections/verify-across-every-partition-the-write-touched.md===\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: Verify Across Every Partition the Write Touched\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/duckdb-snapshot-merge-by-primary-key\"\n  - \"concepts/mac-owned-data-pulls-sleep-lateness\"\n  - \"concepts/baylor-tech-inventory-analysis\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-22.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-22\nupdated: 2026-09-24\n---\n\n# Connection: Verify Across Every Partition the Write Touched\n\n## The Connection\n\nThree checks on 2026-09-22 returned accurate answers to questions narrower than the ones being asked. The PFF snapshot load was verified by comparing the 2026 season and reported success while 12,750 rows had been deleted from 2024–2025. The Mac-owned data pulls were verified by exit code and data currency — both genuinely clean — while two of three jobs had run an hour off schedule. And the same day's earlier inventory verification, from the 2026-09-07 pattern, established the counter-example: it checked the raw `.xlsx`, compared md5s across two locations, *and* searched the whole home folder for a newer export before concluding nothing had changed.\n\n## Key Insight\n\nThe scope of a verification is chosen, usually implicitly, by whatever is cheapest and most salient to sample — and salience runs exactly opposite to risk. The current season is the most visible partition and the one the operation was designed to get right; the historical seasons are invisible and are precisely where the two machines' holdings had silently diverged. A load that writes N partitions requires N checks, not one check on the partition you were thinking about.\n\nThe rule generalizes past row counts. \"Partition\" means any dimension along which the operation's effect varies: seasons in a database load, but also the *time* dimension for a scheduled job (exit code covers whether it ran, not when), and *location* for a file that exists in two places (a filename match is not a content match). In each case the narrow check is not wrong — it is true and insufficient, which is the dangerous combination, because a truthful pass is read as an all-clear.\n\nThe practical test is to state, before verifying, what the operation could have changed, and then confirm that the check's scope covers that set. The PFF load could have changed four tables across three seasons; the check covered one season. The pull could have changed data content *and* arrival time; the check covered content. Only the inventory verification enumerated its own possible-change surface first and then built three independent checks to span it.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- **PFF load (2026-09-22):** the first verification \"said it worked — that was wrong. The first check only compared 2026, so it missed the loss.\" 12,750 Mac-only 2024–2025 `pff_premium` rows across `video_plays`, `team_play`, `quarterback_charting`, and `rushing` were silently deleted. Recorded lesson: \"Row-count checks must cover every season the load touches.\"\n- **Mac data pulls (2026-09-22):** all three pulls exited 0 with data current to 9/21 — a fully passing content check — while GPS ran on time and Perch/VALD finished ~17:36–17:39 against 16:35/16:40 schedules, Perch timing out twice first. The temporal partition was outside the check's scope.\n- **Tech inventory (2026-09-07, the counter-example):** \"has anything changed?\" was answered by reading the raw `.xlsx` rather than the cleaned CSV, comparing local and OneDrive copies by md5 rather than filename, and searching the home folder for a newer export — three checks spanning content, replication, and existence. That breadth is what exposed the Microsoft-Forms-versus-Google-Forms drift, which no single check would have surfaced.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/duckdb-snapshot-merge-by-primary-key]] — The 2026-only check that missed a 12,750-row deletion in older seasons.\n- [[concepts/mac-owned-data-pulls-sleep-lateness]] — A clean content verification over a schedule that was not honored.\n- [[concepts/baylor-tech-inventory-analysis]] — The three-independent-checks counter-example that caught a platform mismatch.\n- [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]] — The reporting-side twin: a field answering accurately on a narrower predicate.\n- [[connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output]] — Distrusting the intermediate layer; this article addresses how *wide* the ground-truth check must be.\n- [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]] — The set-membership form of the same error: what was never in the checked set cannot fail.\n- [[connections/every-layer-reported-healthy-except-the-destination]] — Locally-truthful stage predicates that do not compose into end-to-end assurance.\n\n===END ARTICLE===\n\n===ARTICLE connections/a-guard-that-cannot-run-fails-open.md===\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: A Guard That Cannot Run Fails Open\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/claude-code-stack-config-audit-2026-09-22\"\n  - \"concepts/dcg-guard-false-positives-native-tool-workaround\"\n  - \"concepts/mac-owned-data-pulls-sleep-lateness\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-22.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-22\nupdated: 2026-09-24\n---\n\n# Connection: A Guard That Cannot Run Fails Open\n\n## The Connection\n\nThe 2026-09-22 stack audit found the dcg safety hook dead on the Windows laptop — 913 failures, because Windows settings point at the Mac path `/Users/kristinarash/.local/bin/dcg`. The same day's data-pull review found that the Perch and VALD failure-alert paths had still never fired and remain unproven. Both are protective mechanisms whose absence produces exactly the same observable signal as their success: silence.\n\n## Key Insight\n\nA guard and an alert are both defined by what they do in the bad case, which means neither is exercised by the good case. When such a mechanism is broken, the system does not degrade visibly — it degrades into looking fine. The operator's model still includes the protection, so the perceived risk drops precisely when the actual risk rises.\n\nThis inverts the usual debugging heuristic. For a productive component, absence of output is a symptom; for a protective component, absence of output is the expected steady state, and the only way to distinguish \"working and quiet\" from \"dead and quiet\" is to deliberately produce the condition it guards against — or, failing that, to check the mechanism's own liveness independently of its verdicts. The 913 failure records are what made the dcg case discoverable at all: the hook was loud about failing to run, and nobody was reading that channel. The Perch/VALD alerts have no such record, so their state is not merely unknown but unknowable from the outside.\n\nThe practical consequence is that a protective mechanism needs two things a productive one does not: a liveness signal separate from its findings, and a periodic deliberate trip. Neither is expensive. Both were absent here.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- **dcg on Windows:** 913 recorded failures from a Mac-absolute path in Windows settings; \"the dcg guard checks nothing there.\" The guard was nominally configured in both places, and its configuration was the only thing anyone had checked.\n- **Perch and VALD alerts:** \"No alerts fired, so the Perch and VALD failure-alert paths are still unproven.\" The 2026-09-22 run was late but ultimately successful, so it exercised neither the alert nor a genuine no-alert case; the item was carried forward as \"test the Perch/VALD failure alerts.\"\n- **Precedent — the morning-briefing failure email** died because its send path used the same dead venv it was meant to report on, going six days undetected; and the `-DryRun` guard in `daily-backup.ps1` printed \"committed / pushed\" for operations it never performed, because the log line sat outside the guard it described.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-code-stack-config-audit-2026-09-22]] — Where the 913 dcg failures and the Mac-path-in-Windows-settings root cause were found.\n- [[concepts/dcg-guard-false-positives-native-tool-workaround]] — The guard in question, extensively documented on the Mac where it does run.\n- [[concepts/mac-owned-data-pulls-sleep-lateness]] — The untested Perch and VALD alert paths.\n- [[connections/preconditions-that-check-the-wrong-thing]] — Guards detached from their own results or coupled to the wrong property.\n- [[concepts/failure-notification-shared-dependency]] — An alert path that shared the monitored system's broken dependency.\n- [[connections/alerting-path-must-not-share-the-monitored-systems-budget]] — The general rule for alert-path independence.\n- [[concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11]] — The `-DryRun` logging-outside-the-guard defect, the same failure one layer up.\n- [[connections/the-monitor-is-the-defect]] — When the monitoring layer, not the monitored system, is what is broken.\n\n===END ARTICLE===\n\n===INDEX===\n| [[concepts/mac-owned-data-pulls-sleep-lateness]] | First Mac-owned day for GPS/Perch/VALD pulls: sleep at 3:45 PM made Perch/VALD ~1 hour late (2 Perch timeouts), all exited 0; `pmset` wake declined with a missing-not-late revisit trigger | 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/duckdb-snapshot-merge-by-primary-key]] | `load_snapshot.py` silently deleted 12,750 Mac-only 2024–2025 rows because a 2026-only check passed; rewritten test-first to merge by primary key, restored from its own pre-load backup | 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/claude-code-stack-config-audit-2026-09-22]] | 50-session + 303-session audit: 5 zero-use plugins proposed for disabling, `: \"` in YAML breaking a whole SKILL.md header, dcg hook dead on Windows (913 failures, Mac path), skill-router 68 s worst case | 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge]] | 146-repo sync: union merge for append-only transcript logs, backup manifest not auto-discovering `TOS_Evidence_Brief`, Tier A memory not crossing machines, `compile.py` (~$1.28) left as the user's call | 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[connections/verify-across-every-partition-the-write-touched]] | A 2026-only row check, an exit-code-only pull check, and the three-check inventory counter-example: verification scope must span every dimension the operation could change | 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[connections/a-guard-that-cannot-run-fails-open]] | The dead Windows dcg hook and the never-fired Perch/VALD alerts both look identical to success; protective mechanisms need a liveness signal and a deliberate periodic trip | 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n===END INDEX===\n\n===LOG===\n## [2026-09-24T19:47:14-05:00] compile | 2026-09-22.md\n- Source: daily/2026-09-22.md\n- Articles created: [[concepts/mac-owned-data-pulls-sleep-lateness]], [[concepts/duckdb-snapshot-merge-by-primary-key]], [[concepts/claude-code-stack-config-audit-2026-09-22]], [[concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge]], [[connections/verify-across-every-partition-the-write-touched]], [[connections/a-guard-that-cannot-run-fails-open]]\n- Articles updated: (none)\n===END LOG==="}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":104358,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":15212,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":104358,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":15212,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":104358,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":104358},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfPEdmyfe93m9Cf8yNf3p","type":"assistant","uuid":"409f791b-fafb-4e04-9503-91f6ab9b4861","timestamp":"2026-09-25T00:50:12.257Z","effort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"efd82c20-18dd-40c4-b3d9-e70a9a007529","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"409f791b-fafb-4e04-9503-91f6ab9b4861","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"deferred_tools_record","entries":[{"name":"mcp__claude_ai_Claude_Docs__batch","description":"Create a doc, or apply several operations to one doc atomically.","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"batch":{"type":"array"},"container":{"type":"object","properties":{"kind":{"type":"string"},"id":{"type":"string"},"create":{"type":"object"}},"required":["kind"]},"verbose":{"type":"boolean"},"opId":{"type":"string"}}},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"mcp__claude_ai_Claude_Docs__guide","description":"Docs guides: topic.instructions repeats the server instructions. Read it only if your client dropped them. Also topic.<name>, refusal.<code>. After a doc's birth → [\"topic.index\"].","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"items":{"type":"array","description":"topic.<name> (instructions, index, editing, tabs, comments, charts, chart-definition, uploads, sharing, skill) or refusal.<code>; several per call is fine."}}},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"mcp__claude_ai_Claude_Docs__update","description":"Edit a tab's contents, rename a doc or tab, or change a stored value.","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"ref":{"type":"object","properties":{"object":{"type":"string","enum":["project","file","node","utterance","enum"]},"id":{"type":"string"}},"required":["object","id"]},"engine":{"type":"string"},"payload":{"anyOf":[{"type":"object"},{"type":"string"}]},"container":{"type":"object","properties":{"kind":{"type":"string"},"id":{"type":"string"},"version":{"type":"string"}},"required":["kind","id"]},"verbose":{"type":"boolean"},"opId":{"type":"string"},"answering":{"type":"string","maxLength":64}},"required":["ref","payload"]},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"mcp__claude_ai_Firecrawl__firecrawl_scrape","description":"\nScrape one URL and return its content, or execute catalogued Alexandria capabilities. URL mode returns markdown by default, or HTML, links, screenshots, branding data, a targeted answer, or JSON matching a supplied schema, plus page metadata. Firecrawl may serve recently indexed content; set `maxAge: 0` for a live fetch. A successful response does not by itself confirm the page is still current. Browser actions can change the live page when interactive actions are enabled, and a named browser profile can load saved session data and overwrite its stored state.\n\nBefore scraping the same fields from several pages, first run `firecrawl_search` with `sources` unset (or `firecrawl_find_tools`): a matching Alexandria provider returns those fields as typed records in one call.\n\nAlexandria mode: pass `alexandria` instead of `url`; the `alexandria` and `requestId` parameters describe batching, retries, errors and provider terms. Alexandria execution is billed at the capability's listed price and needs a team with Alexandria enabled.\n","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"url":{"type":"string","format":"uri"},"formats":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["markdown","html","rawHtml","screenshot","links","summary","changeTracking","branding","json","query","audio"]}},"jsonOptions":{"type":"object","properties":{"prompt":{"type":"string"},"schema":{"type":"object","propertyNames":{"type":"string"},"additionalProperties":false}},"additionalProperties":false},"queryOptions":{"type":"object","properties":{"prompt":{"type":"string","maxLength":10000},"mode":{"default":"freeform","type":"string","enum":["directQuote","freeform"]}},"required":["prompt"],"additionalProperties":false},"screenshotOptions":{"type":"object","properties":{"fullPage":{"type":"boolean"},"quality":{"type":"number"},"viewport":{"type":"object","properties":{"width":{"type":"number"},"height":{"type":"number"}},"required":["width","height"],"additionalProperties":false}},"additionalProperties":false},"parsers":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["pdf"]}},"pdfOptions":{"type":"object","properties":{"maxPages":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":10000}},"additionalProperties":false},"onlyMainContent":{"type":"boolean"},"redactPII":{"type":"boolean"},"includeTags":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"excludeTags":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"waitFor":{"type":"number"},"mobile":{"type":"boolean"},"skipTlsVerification":{"type":"boolean"},"removeBase64Images":{"type":"boolean"},"location":{"type":"object","properties":{"country":{"type":"string"},"languages":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}}},"additionalProperties":false},"storeInCache":{"type":"boolean"},"zeroDataRetention":{"type":"boolean"},"maxAge":{"type":"number"},"lockdown":{"type":"boolean"},"proxy":{"type":"string","enum":["basic","stealth","enhanced","auto"]},"profile":{"type":"object","properties":{"name":{"type":"string"},"saveChanges":{"type":"boolean"}},"required":["name"],"additionalProperties":false},"timeout":{"description":"Execution timeout in milliseconds.","type":"integer","exclusiveMinimum":0,"maximum":9007199254740991},"requestId":{"description":"Alexandria execution ID. Reuse the returned ID for retries of the identical payload, never a new ID to bypass pending or uncertain execution; generated when omitted. For potentially large workflow results, supply and preserve one before execution. Errors relay a code and chargeId: request_in_flight (409) retry the same requestId later; request_unresolved (503) keep the requestId for reconciliation, never mint a new one; duplicate_request (409) the requestId belongs to a different payload; unknown_provider (404), insufficient_credits (402) and billing_unavailable (503) mean nothing executed.","type":"string","pattern":"^[A-Za-z0-9._:-]{1,128}$"},"alexandria":{"description":"Execute catalogued Alexandria capabilities instead of scraping a URL. Exactly one of url or alexandria. One {provider, capability, options} object or an array of 1-10, found through firecrawl_search or firecrawl_find_tools. Each call may include version to pin a published workflow; omitting it uses latest. Only timeout also applies at the top level. Read the selected contract before executing: required inputs and requiresOneOf groups (at least one member per group), example.request/example.response when present, and response.key (do not assume records is the result key). Follow the declared pagination input and response cursor, preserving filters; catalogue next is separate from provider pagination. Returns per-capability results in data.alexandria with data, records, or an error with a code; check each item even when the outer response succeeds. If a response provides nextTool, follow it to read a large result instead of repeating a successful provider call. Needs an API key on a team with Alexandria enabled. A terms-gated provider returns THIRD_PARTY_DATA_TERMS_REQUIRED (403) with requiresAction.url: follow the returned terms/show and terms/accept calls through this tool, accepting only after explicit user authorization for the reviewed version and digest; an organization admin can instead accept at the dashboard URL. Retry only after confirmed acceptance.","anyOf":[{"type":"object","properties":{"provider":{"description":"Provider slug, e.g. \"fred\".","type":"string","minLength":1},"capability":{"description":"Capability address as returned by search or discover, e.g. \"series/observations\".","type":"string","minLength":1},"version":{"description":"Optional published workflow version. Omit to use the latest version.","type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":128},"options":{"description":"Capability options as declared by its contract.","type":"object","propertyNames":{"type":"string"},"additionalProperties":{}}},"required":["provider","capability"]},{"minItems":1,"maxItems":10,"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"provider":{"description":"Provider slug, e.g. \"fred\".","type":"string","minLength":1},"capability":{"description":"Capability address as returned by search or discover, e.g. \"series/observations\".","type":"string","minLength":1},"version":{"description":"Optional published workflow version. Omit to use the latest version.","type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":128},"options":{"description":"Capability options as declared by its contract.","type":"object","propertyNames":{"type":"string"},"additionalProperties":{}}},"required":["provider","capability"]}}]},"toolDetail":{"description":"URL domain discovery detail: summary by default, compact returns provider/capability/description, full includes contracts.","type":"string","enum":["compact","summary","full"]},"domainTools":{"description":"URL mode only: include domain-matched Alexandria tools for the page in tools on the returned document.","type":"boolean"}},"$schema":"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#","additionalProperties":false},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"mcp__claude_ai_Firecrawl__firecrawl_search","description":"\nSearch web and specialized indexes, returning ranked results with query-relevant highlights. Each web result is a title, URL, and description. Operators include quoted phrases, `-term`, `site:host`, `inurl:term`, `intitle:term`, and `related:host`; the set is non-exhaustive. `includeDomains` and `excludeDomains` are mutually exclusive hostname filters; categories limit result types to `research`, `pdf`, or `developer`.\n\nFor a programming question, add `categories: [\"developer\"]`. It searches an index of public repositories, GitHub issues, merged pull requests, repository READMEs, and code documentation, and returns the results in `data.web` with `category: \"developer\"`.\n\nAuthenticated search combines web results, semantic tool summaries and domain matches. Use sources: [\"alexandria\"] for semantic tools only, or sources: [\"web\"] for web only. domainTools: false disables domain matching. Tool matches describe available structured-data capabilities, not executed data. toolDetail: \"compact\" (default) returns only provider, capability and description; \"summary\" adds metadata; \"full\" includes their input and output contracts. Execute a matched tool through firecrawl_scrape with an alexandria body; use firecrawl_find_tools to browse the catalogue or read a full contract.\n\nReturns result groups in `data` and an operation `id`.\n","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"toolDetail":{"description":"Compact by default. Compact returns only provider, capability and description; full includes contracts. Inspect selected compact tools with firecrawl_find_tools providers and capabilities.","type":"string","enum":["compact","summary","full"]},"query":{"description":"Query for web and semantic tool discovery. Operators include quoted phrases, `-term`, `site:host`, `inurl:term`, `intitle:term`, and `related:host`; the set is non-exhaustive. Catalogue browsing is available through firecrawl_find_tools.","type":"string","minLength":1},"domainTools":{"description":"Include domain-matched tools for result URLs. Defaults to true when Alexandria is combined with web, news or images; semantic-only search leaves domain matching off.","type":"boolean"},"highlights":{"description":"Return query-relevant page excerpts for web and news results when available (default). Highlights appear in web `description` and news `snippet`; otherwise, original snippets are returned. Set to false to keep the original search snippets.","type":"boolean"},"limit":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":100},"tbs":{"type":"string"},"filter":{"type":"string"},"location":{"type":"string"},"includeDomains":{"description":"Hostnames to restrict results to. Mutually exclusive with excludeDomains.","type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":253,"pattern":"^(?:[a-z0-9](?:[a-z0-9-]{0,61}[a-z0-9])?\\.)+[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,61}[a-z0-9]$"}},"excludeDomains":{"description":"Hostnames to leave out of results. Mutually exclusive with includeDomains.","type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":253,"pattern":"^(?:[a-z0-9](?:[a-z0-9-]{0,61}[a-z0-9])?\\.)+[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,61}[a-z0-9]$"}},"sources":{"description":"Search sources; authenticated sessions default to web + alexandria, keyless sessions to web only. Passing sources without alexandria in it (for example [\"web\"] or [\"news\"]) excludes Alexandria provider matches; omit sources unless you specifically need web-only or news-only results, or include \"alexandria\" alongside them. Use [\"alexandria\"] alone for provider discovery without web results.","type":"array","items":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["web","images","news","alexandria","exchange"]},{"type":"object","properties":{"type":{"type":"string","enum":["web","images","news","alexandria","exchange"]}},"required":["type"],"additionalProperties":false}]}},"categories":{"description":"Limit results to specific source types. `research` restricts ordinary web results to research-affiliated websites and returns page snippets, which is separate from the `firecrawl_research_*` tools that search paper abstracts and full text across biomedical (PubMed, bioRxiv, medRxiv) and arXiv literature; `pdf` searches PDF results; `developer` searches an index built for coding agents over public repositories, GitHub issues, merged pull requests, repository READMEs, and code documentation. `developer` returns hits in `data.web` with `category: \"developer\"`; the other categories also filter `data.web`.","type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["research","pdf","developer"]}},"enterprise":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["default","anon","zdr"]}}},"required":["query"],"$schema":"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#","additionalProperties":false},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"6bfac168-66c0-4d52-883a-8da7cbe5fa44","timestamp":"2026-09-25T00:50:12.422Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"efd82c20-18dd-40c4-b3d9-e70a9a007529","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
